/** Original supplemental teaching. Baseline courses remain separate bundle members.
 * No online result records a physical demonstration, medical clearance or issuer award. */
const osha = n => `https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/${n.split('.')[0]}/${n}`;
const data = {};
export const programs = data;
export const workplaceCurricula = data;
export default data;
function program(id, sources, coverage, limitations, rows) {
 const lessons = rows.map((r,i)=>({id:`${id}_workplace_${i+1}`,title:r[0],body:r[1],links:sources,fileIds:[]}));
 const checks = rows.flatMap((r,i)=>r[2].map((q,j)=>({id:`${id}_workplace_${i+1}_check${j+1}`,lessonId:lessons[i].id,prompt:q[0],answer:q[1],distractors:q.slice(2,5),explanation:q[5]})));
 data[id]={lessons,checks,review:{sources,coverage,limitations}};
}
program('OG39',[osha('1910.95')],
 ['Exposure interpretation','Noise controls and protector fit','Audiometry and reporting','Fit and program-use assessment'],
 ['Actual exposure monitoring, protector attenuation assessment and audiometric program remain employer deliverables. General-industry thresholds are not automatically every jurisdiction’s rule.'],[
 ['Understand noise dose rather than judging by discomfort',`Noise can damage hearing without immediate pain. Duration, level and repeated exposure matter, and a worker may adapt to the sound without becoming protected. Ringing or muffled hearing after work is a reason to report and obtain appropriate evaluation, not proof that the ears will always recover. Communication difficulty also creates operational risks when alarms and instructions cannot be understood.

Under OSHA's general-industry noise rule, the hearing-conservation action level is an eight-hour time-weighted average of 85 dBA, while the permissible-exposure framework uses its own limits and exchange rate. Do not treat the action level as a universal safe/unsafe line or interchange the OSHA method with another organization's recommended method. The employer's competent exposure assessment determines inclusion and required controls for the actual work pattern. A brief area reading does not automatically describe a worker's full-shift exposure.

Worked case: a mechanic alternates between a compressor enclosure and a quiet workshop. Measuring only the workshop misses the high-noise task; measuring only a moment at the compressor may also misrepresent the total. Use appropriate monitoring of representative tasks and duration, including changes in equipment or production. Do not remove protectors in the noisy area to hear a conversation; move to a safe communication location or use the approved communication system while maintaining necessary protection.`,[
 ['No pain from noise proves what?','Nothing about whether exposure can damage hearing.','The noise is safe.','Protectors are unnecessary.','Monitoring is unnecessary.','Noise injury can occur without immediate pain.'],
 ['The cited general-industry hearing-conservation action level is?','85 dBA as an eight-hour time-weighted average.','85 dBA as any instantaneous sound.','100 dBA for every shift length.','A universal limit for all jurisdictions.','The action level has a defined averaging basis and scope.'],
 ['A quiet workshop reading covers a worker’s compressor task. Correct?','No; representative exposure includes the actual task pattern.','Yes if both areas share an employer.','Yes if the workshop is larger.','Yes if the meter is new.','Exposure assessment must represent where and how the person works.']]],
 ['Reduce noise and fit protection that works for the person',`Engineering controls can reduce noise at the source or along its path: maintenance, quieter equipment, enclosure, isolation and acoustic treatment may be appropriate. Administrative changes can reduce exposure duration when suitable. Hearing protectors supplement the required control strategy; distributing earplugs does not establish that noise has been adequately controlled or that each person receives sufficient attenuation.

Selection considers measured exposure, required attenuation, comfort, compatibility and communication needs. A package noise-reduction rating is not the exact reduction every wearer receives in the field. The employer must evaluate adequacy using the applicable method and ensure proper initial fitting and correct use. Earplugs require the model's insertion technique. Earmuff seals can be disturbed by eyewear, hard-hat attachments, hair or damaged cushions. Wearing two products does not mean simply adding their labeled ratings.

Worked case: a worker's earmuff cushion rests on a thick spectacle arm and leaves a gap. The correct response is to adjust the compatible equipment arrangement or obtain a suitable alternative and verify fit, not to assume the original laboratory rating still applies. Inspect protectors and replace worn or dirty components under the instructions. Store clean reusable devices so they do not carry contaminants into the ear. Practice fitting in a clean, safe area before relying on the device in the noisy task.`,[
 ['Are two labeled protector ratings simply added?','No.','Yes always.','Yes if the products match.','Yes below100 dBA.','Combined attenuation needs the appropriate evaluation method.'],
 ['A spectacle arm breaks an earmuff seal. What next?','Correct the compatible fit before relying on protection.','Assume the labeled rating still applies.','Push the earmuff forward and ignore it.','Remove eye protection in the hazard.','A seal gap can reduce actual attenuation.'],
 ['What should precede relying solely on PPE?','Consider and implement required feasible noise controls.','Only purchase more plugs.','Only issue a warning sign.','Only shorten the training.','Noise reduction at the source or path reduces dependence on personal fit.']]],
 ['Use audiometry to find change, not to excuse exposure',`Audiometric testing supports the hearing-conservation program by establishing and comparing hearing thresholds over time under the required conditions. A baseline and subsequent tests are not a guarantee that current exposure is safe. Workers should understand the purpose, preparation and follow-up and be able to attend without replacing the test with a self-reported impression of hearing ability. Clinical interpretation and personal medical information belong with the appropriate professionals and protected records.

The employer must address a standard threshold shift through the applicable notification and follow-up requirements. That can include fitting or refitting protectors, training and referral where appropriate. Do not announce an individual's hearing results in a crew meeting. A program-level review can discuss exposure controls and fit issues without identifying private medical details.

Worked case: several workers report that a repaired compressor sounds noticeably louder, while one worker has a recent satisfactory audiogram. The audiogram does not validate the changed machine. Report the change, reassess exposure and correct the control problem. A worker with ringing or difficulty understanding speech should report it promptly rather than waiting for the next annual training. The employer should examine the actual work, protection and monitoring, not assume the worker is at fault because earplugs were issued. Record corrective actions and verify that the equipment or process change is reflected in the program.`,[
 ['A satisfactory audiogram proves a changed noisy machine is safe. Correct?','No.','Yes for one year.','Yes if protectors were issued.','Yes if the worker feels fine.','Hearing-test results do not replace current exposure assessment.'],
 ['Who should interpret individual audiometric findings?','The appropriate qualified professionals under the program.','The course platform from a quiz.','Any coworker.','The loudest supervisor.','Clinical interpretation and follow-up require appropriate expertise.'],
 ['How should an individual’s hearing result be discussed?','Through the appropriate confidential process.','Publicly at muster.','On a visible badge.','In a general team chat.','Program improvement does not require exposing private results.']]],
 ['Observe correct fit and program use',`Use the actual protectors and compatible PPE in a clean training area. The evaluator explains the model's fitting method, demonstrates it and observes the learner repeat it. For insert devices, assess the correct preparation, insertion and fit checks specified by the manufacturer. For earmuffs, inspect cushions and headband condition and check the seal with the other PPE worn. Where fit testing is used, record the real result and method; do not manufacture an attenuation value from visual inspection.

Give the learner a workplace case with a changed noise source, a damaged protector and a communication problem. They should identify the need for reporting and reassessment, obtain a usable protector and use a safe communication method without removing protection in the exposure. Ask where monitoring results, audiometric arrangements and replacement supplies can be accessed.

Passing requires correct fit, care, limitations and reporting decisions. A persistent broken seal, use of damaged protection or removing protection to communicate in a hazardous area is a critical error. Teach and reassess. Record the model and observed skills separately from exposure monitoring and medical records. Employees in the covered hearing-conservation program receive annual training, updated to reflect protective equipment and work changes. An online completion record does not establish a personal attenuation rating, a clinical hearing result or the effectiveness of the site's engineering controls.`,[
 ['Can visual inspection alone generate a measured personal attenuation value?','No.','Yes if fit looks tight.','Yes from the package rating.','Yes after a quiz.','A measured fit result requires the actual measurement method.'],
 ['Which practical observation requires correction?','A persistent gap in the earmuff seal.','A clean storage case.','Correct plug insertion.','Reporting a louder machine.','A poor seal undermines protective performance.'],
 ['Covered hearing-conservation training occurs at least?','Annually.','Once at hiring only.','Every five years.','Only after hearing loss.','The general-industry program includes annual employee training.']]]
]);

program('OG40',[osha('1926.1153'),osha('1910.1053')],
 ['Task and material recognition','Construction Table1 versus exposure assessment','Housekeeping and surveillance','Control implementation evaluation'],
 ['Actual exposure data, Table1 task match, written plan, competent-person assignment where applicable and medical surveillance remain site-specific. No asbestos or lead qualification is implied.'],[
 ['Connect the material to the dust-generating task',`Respirable crystalline silica can be generated when silica-containing materials are cut, ground, drilled, crushed or otherwise disturbed. The respirable fraction is small enough to reach deep into the lungs and may not be visible. Absence of a large dust cloud does not establish a safe exposure. Health effects include silicosis and other serious diseases addressed by the applicable standard; the worker needs to understand the risk without trying to diagnose themselves from symptoms.

Identify both material and method. A concrete slab, sand handling or abrasive process can present different exposure patterns. An SDS or survey may identify the material, but a bulk silica percentage does not directly equal a worker's airborne exposure. Task duration, enclosure, tool, dust control and nearby work affect the exposure. Historical data must actually represent the current conditions if used to support a decision.

Worked case: a crew changes from wet outdoor cutting to dry cutting inside a small building. The material is unchanged, but the method and environment are not. Stop and reassess the applicable control approach before continuing. Determine whether construction or general-industry requirements apply to the work rather than applying construction Table1 to every oilfield task. The worker should be able to name the dust-generating step, the people potentially exposed and the control that must operate while the task occurs.`,[
 ['No visible dust cloud proves what?','Nothing conclusive about respirable silica exposure.','Zero exposure.','No need for controls.','Only harmless particles remain.','Respirable particles may not be visible.'],
 ['Bulk silica percentage equals airborne worker exposure. Correct?','No.','Yes directly.','Yes outdoors.','Yes with a new tool.','Airborne exposure also depends on task and control conditions.'],
 ['Wet outdoor cutting becomes dry indoor cutting. What next?','Reassess and establish the applicable controls before proceeding.','Reuse the old decision unchanged.','Only update the job title.','Assume the same material means the same risk.','Method and environment materially affect exposure.']]],
 ['Implement the entire specified control method',`Construction Table1 is a task-specific control route, not a list of suggestions. To use it, the actual task must match the listed equipment and conditions and the specified controls must be fully and properly implemented. Required respiratory protection can depend on task duration and whether work is indoors or enclosed. If the task does not fit or the required method is not implemented, use the applicable alternative exposure-assessment and control route rather than borrowing one favorable line from the table.

Water delivery must reach the cutting or grinding point as intended. Dust collection requires the appropriate shroud, airflow, filtration and maintenance for the specified tool and method. A vacuum hose nearby does not establish effective source capture. Check the manufacturer instructions and the actual required method; do not substitute a household vacuum for an appropriate dust-control system. Respirators, where required, bring the respiratory-program prerequisites as well.

Worked case: a saw's water feed clogs halfway through cutting. The control has failed. Stop the dust-generating work, correct the feed safely and confirm operation before resuming. A worker cannot claim Table1 compliance because the saw was designed for water while operating it dry. Similarly, a dust collector with a blocked filter may look connected yet fail to capture dust. The competent person where required must identify these implementation problems and take corrective action, not merely check that equipment is present.`,[
 ['Can only the convenient parts of Table1 be used to claim its control route?','No; the matching method must be fully and properly implemented.','Yes with a respirator nearby.','Yes for short tasks.','Yes if the tool model is listed.','The specified conditions operate as a complete method.'],
 ['The saw’s water feed stops. What next?','Stop the dust-generating task and restore the required control.','Continue because the tool supports wet cutting.','Finish faster.','Rely on the old checklist.','Design capability does not equal operating control.'],
 ['A connected vacuum guarantees source capture. Correct?','No; suitability, airflow, filtration and condition matter.','Yes for any vacuum.','Yes if the hose is long.','Yes if it makes noise.','A control must function to the actual method requirements.']]],
 ['Housekeeping, written plans and medical interfaces',`Dust on floors and equipment can become airborne again during cleanup. The silica rules restrict dry sweeping or dry brushing where it could contribute to exposure unless safer methods are not feasible, and restrict compressed-air cleaning under the stated conditions. Use the applicable wet or HEPA-filtered methods and the employer's plan. Do not solve a dusty floor by blowing contamination into another worker's breathing zone.

The written exposure-control plan identifies tasks, controls and housekeeping arrangements. Construction also includes the required competent-person and access-control provisions. General-industry requirements include their own regulated-area and program elements where applicable. Know which framework governs the task and who can correct a failed control. The plan must describe actual work rather than a generic statement that masks are available.

Medical surveillance has different eligibility provisions in construction and general industry. The employer must determine applicability from the actual exposure or respirator-use conditions under the governing standard; do not invent a single trigger for every worker. Clinical examinations and results remain with appropriate professionals and protected records, while workers receive the required information and access.

Worked case: a crew completes controlled cutting, then dry sweeps the debris in an occupied room. The cleanup can recreate exposure after the main tool stops. Use the approved cleaning method, manage access and waste and verify the area is ready for handback. Task completion does not automatically mean exposure has ended.`,[
 ['Can cleanup recreate silica exposure after cutting stops?','Yes.','No once the tool is off.','Only outdoors.','Only if the debris is warm.','Settled dust can be resuspended.'],
 ['What should replace casual compressed-air dust blowing?','The applicable approved controlled cleaning method.','A stronger air nozzle.','Nozzle use while holding breath.','Blowing toward another room.','Housekeeping must avoid creating avoidable airborne exposure.'],
 ['Are medical-surveillance eligibility rules identical across both silica standards?','No; apply the governing standard to actual conditions.','Yes universally.','Only job title matters.','A quiz score determines eligibility.','Construction and general-industry provisions differ.']]],
 ['Evaluate whether the control actually operates',`The evaluator uses the assigned tool and control system in a safe demonstration. The learner identifies the applicable task method, assembles or checks the shroud, water feed or collector, explains inspection and maintenance and recognizes the required respiratory interface. Use simulation or a nonhazardous material where appropriate; do not create uncontrolled silica dust to test a learner's reaction.

Present a blocked water line, disconnected hose, overloaded collector or changed indoor work location. The learner must recognize that the selected control basis may no longer apply and stop or obtain reassessment. Ask them to choose the correct housekeeping method and identify who maintains the written plan and, where applicable, acts as the competent person. The evaluation should distinguish knowing a table exists from correctly implementing its actual matching row.

Passing requires task identification, functioning controls, stop decisions, safe housekeeping and appropriate reporting. Continuing dry operation after a required water failure, sweeping exposure-generating dust without the permitted basis or claiming monitoring data from an unrelated task is a critical failure. Record the equipment, method, conditions, evaluator and observed outcomes, then remediate and reassess as needed. This learning record cannot establish an exposure concentration, complete a medical examination or qualify the learner for asbestos or lead work merely because those substances appear in a supporting course. Keep the silica authorization specific to the actual demonstrated assignment.`,[
 ['Which observation is a critical failure?','Continuing after failure of the required water control.','Stopping for a disconnected hose.','Checking the written plan.','Selecting a suitable cleaning method.','The control route depends on functioning controls during the task.'],
 ['Can unrelated historical monitoring establish today’s exposure automatically?','No.','Yes if lower.','Yes from the same employer.','Yes if printed in color.','Data must represent the relevant conditions.'],
 ['Does silica course completion qualify asbestos work?','No.','Yes because both involve dust.','Yes with a respirator.','Yes after this assessment.','Different substances and roles have distinct qualification requirements.']]]
]);

program('OG41',['https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/motor-vehicle/about/'],
 ['Journey necessity and fitness','Space and speed management','Remote roads reversing and breakdown','Vehicle-specific practical evaluation'],
 ['NIOSH source supports prevention principles; detailed driving exercises are original instructional applications. Applicable licensing, carrier rules and actual vehicle evaluation remain separate.'],[
 ['Make the journey decision before the driver is committed',`Driving is a work task with an exposure that begins when the trip is planned. Ask whether the trip is necessary, whether the task can be combined or performed remotely and whether the driver, vehicle and route are suitable. Schedule enough time for safe travel and rest rather than rewarding the fastest arrival. A customer deadline does not change stopping distance, fatigue or road conditions.

The driver needs the appropriate license and employer authorization for the vehicle and task. Vehicle condition, load securement, trailer configuration and road access must match the assignment. A passenger-car history does not prove safe handling of a loaded pickup and trailer on an unpaved lease road. Identify weather, communication gaps, fuel, safe stopping locations and the response to a missed check-in before departure.

Worked case: a worker finishes a long night shift and is asked to drive several hours home in a company vehicle. Coffee and an open window are not reliable substitutes for sleep. Report fatigue and use the employer's safe alternative, such as rest, another qualified driver or a changed travel arrangement. Do not treat a signed journey form as a fitness assessment. Drivers should also report impairment from illness, alcohol, drugs or medication through the appropriate process, without being pressured to disclose private clinical details in a public dispatch message.`,[
 ['A deadline makes an unsafe travel schedule acceptable. Correct?','No.','Yes for a customer emergency.','Yes with a signed form.','Yes if the driver volunteers.','Scheduling cannot remove fatigue or road hazards.'],
 ['What is the appropriate response to serious fatigue before a long drive?','Report it and use a safe rest or transport alternative.','Rely on an open window.','Drive faster to finish.','Add louder music.','Fatigue requires an effective change in the travel arrangement.'],
 ['Does ordinary car experience prove loaded trailer proficiency?','No.','Yes automatically.','Yes on private roads.','Yes with a larger engine.','Vehicle and load characteristics require relevant practical ability.']]],
 ['Manage space, speed and attention continuously',`Scan far enough ahead to recognize developing hazards while checking mirrors and nearby traffic. Maintain a following interval appropriate to speed, vehicle, load, weather and visibility. A simple classroom time-gap example is not a universal safe distance. Increase space when stopping performance or visibility worsens, and avoid driving faster than the distance you can see and stop within. A posted maximum does not require driving at that speed.

Distraction can be visual, manual and mental. Set navigation and communication before moving, secure loose objects and stop in a safe place for tasks that take attention from driving. Hands-free conversation can still distract. Seat belts and appropriate occupant restraints are used throughout the trip; a short movement inside a yard is still a movement with collision risk.

Worked case: rain reduces visibility while a loaded truck follows a slow vehicle. The driver reduces speed and increases space rather than staying close to discourage another vehicle from merging. If someone enters the gap, rebuild the gap smoothly. Avoid abrupt steering or braking that can destabilize the load. A missed turn is corrected through a safe route, not a sudden stop or reverse on a live road. The goal is controlled decisions with room to recover, not maintaining an exact arrival estimate at the expense of observation and space.`,[
 ['A posted speed is unsafe for current visibility. What should the driver do?','Reduce speed to suit conditions and stopping sight distance.','Maintain the posted maximum.','Follow another vehicle closely.','Use hazard lights and accelerate.','The maximum limit is not a safe speed for every condition.'],
 ['A vehicle merges into the following gap. What next?','Rebuild safe space smoothly.','Close the gap aggressively.','Brake sharply to punish it.','Race alongside.','Space should be restored without creating another hazard.'],
 ['A missed turn justifies what?','A safe route correction when possible.','Sudden reversing on the road.','An abrupt stop in traffic.','Crossing lanes without checking.','Navigation errors should not become collision events.']]],
 ['Plan reversing, remote roads and breakdowns',`Eliminate unnecessary reversing through parking and route choices where possible. Before reversing, inspect the area and identify pedestrians, obstacles and ground conditions. Use the vehicle's mirrors and cameras as aids, not proof that every blind spot is clear. Where a spotter is used, agree signals and positioning; stop if the spotter is lost from view or communication. Never ask the spotter to stand in a crush zone to improve visibility.

Remote roads add variable traction, soft shoulders, animals, washouts and limited emergency access. Do not enter floodwater or an unknown damaged crossing because a route map says the road continues. Use the journey plan's check-in and overdue response arrangements. A lack of mobile service should be planned for rather than discovered after a breakdown.

Worked case: a loaded vehicle becomes stuck beside a soft shoulder. The driver protects occupants and other traffic, contacts the appropriate assistance and avoids an improvised recovery. Tow points, equipment ratings, stored energy and bystander zones matter. An unrated strap on a tow ball can create a projectile hazard. Do not permit people to stand near a tensioned recovery line. At any breakdown, choose the safest available position and emergency notification method for the circumstances; standing behind the vehicle in moving traffic may be more dangerous than the original mechanical fault.`,[
 ['A reversing spotter disappears. What next?','Stop until safe communication and positioning return.','Continue by camera alone without reassessment.','Accelerate to finish.','Ask the spotter to stand directly behind the bumper.','Lost spotter contact removes an agreed control.'],
 ['A flooded crossing has unknown depth and condition. What should the driver do?','Do not enter; use a safe alternative or await assessment.','Follow the map regardless.','Drive faster through it.','Use another vehicle as a depth gauge.','Unknown flood conditions can conceal severe hazards.'],
 ['What is wrong with improvised vehicle recovery?','Unverified anchors, gear and stored energy can injure people.','Nothing if the engine is powerful.','Only the paint may be damaged.','Only the schedule is affected.','Recovery requires suitable equipment and controlled exclusion.']]],
 ['Evaluate the vehicle and conditions actually assigned',`A practical evaluation should include the vehicle inspection, safe entry and restraints, controls, mirrors, load awareness, maneuvering, reversing and parking. Road evaluation adds observation, space management, speed selection, intersections and response to changing conditions. A trailer or specialized vehicle requires its actual coupling, braking, load and maneuvering skills. Use a qualified evaluator and a controlled route; do not stage an emergency in live traffic to test reactions.

Start in a safe area and progress only when basic handling is demonstrated. Use a desk scenario for severe weather, fatigue or an unsafe recovery rather than creating those hazards. The learner should explain when to stop, whom to contact and how the journey plan changes. Include safe use of a spotter if that is part of the workplace method.

Passing requires consistent observation, restraints, control, suitable speed and recognition of limits. Ignoring a pedestrian, reversing after losing the spotter, using a phone while driving or continuing despite serious impairment is a critical failure. Stop the evaluation safely, provide targeted training and reassess. Record vehicle type, load or trailer scope, route conditions, evaluator and restrictions. The employer determines refresher and reassessment needs from incidents, deficiencies and changed assignments. This VNDRLY result is not a CDL, government license or automatic authorization to drive every vehicle. The final authorization must reflect the actual practical evidence and applicable licensing conditions.`,[
 ['Should an evaluator create an emergency in live traffic?','No; use safe scenarios and controlled methods.','Yes to test courage.','Yes without warning.','Yes on every road test.','Assessment must not create avoidable collision risk.'],
 ['Which behavior is a critical failure?','Using a phone while driving during the evaluation.','Stopping for a pedestrian.','Rejecting an unsafe route.','Using restraints.','Distraction undermines safe control and observation.'],
 ['What must vehicle authorization reflect?','Actual vehicle, task, licensing and demonstrated performance.','Only a generic course title.','Every vehicle owned by the employer.','The driver’s confidence alone.','Practical scope and legal driving eligibility remain specific.']]]
]);
program('OG36',['https://www.phmsa.dot.gov/training/hazmat/training-requirements-industry'],
 ['Function-specific training scope','Shipment consistency','Loading emergencies and security','Employer testing and certification'],
 ['This general module cannot qualify every hazardous-material classification, package, mode or shipping function. Actual HMR task-specific instruction and employer testing/certification remain necessary.'],[
 ['Identify the hazmat function before choosing the training',`A hazmat employee can affect transportation safety through classification, packaging, marking, labeling, preparing shipping papers, loading or driving. The required instruction follows the actual function, not merely the employee's job title. A driver endorsement does not automatically qualify the person to classify a new chemical mixture or select packaging. Equally, a warehouse worker may have hazmat responsibilities without driving a truck.

The federal training framework includes general awareness and familiarization, function-specific training, safety training, security awareness and, when applicable, in-depth security-plan training. Modal and driver requirements may add to this scope. The employer must determine the exact tasks and provide the applicable training, testing and certification. A new employee may perform covered functions before completing required training only within the rule's direct-supervision and time conditions; do not treat a ninety-day completion window as unsupervised authorization.

Worked case: a shipping clerk previously prepared papers for one known product and is now asked to classify waste from a cleaning job. The old training does not establish knowledge of the new mixture or applicable classification method. Hold the shipment and obtain qualified product and regulatory information. Define which decisions the clerk may make and which require another authorized person. The training record should name the function and materials addressed so its boundaries are visible when work changes.`,[
 ['Does a CDL hazmat endorsement qualify every shipping function?','No.','Yes including all classification.','Yes for air shipments automatically.','Yes for any waste.','Driving credentials and other hazmat functions have different requirements.'],
 ['Can a new employee use the initial training window to work unsupervised?','No; the applicable direct-supervision conditions still apply.','Yes for ninety days automatically.','Yes on night shift.','Yes if a manager is reachable by email.','The completion window is not a blanket waiver of supervision.'],
 ['A clerk is assigned unfamiliar waste classification. What next?','Obtain appropriate function-specific information and training before independent work.','Copy a similar product’s description.','Use the cheapest shipping class.','Ask the carrier to guess.','Classification depends on the actual material and requirements.']]],
 ['Reconcile the material, package and shipping description',`Start with reliable material identity and the applicable classification decision. The proper shipping name, identification number, hazard class and packing group where applicable must come from the governing requirements and actual material, not a marketing name alone. An SDS can provide useful information but does not automatically settle every transportation classification or exception. The responsible trained person resolves discrepancies before shipment.

Packaging must be authorized for the material, quantity, mode and conditions. Compatibility, closure instructions, filling limits and required performance markings matter. A UN marking is not permission to place any hazardous material in the container. Follow the manufacturer's closure requirements and retain the necessary evidence. Marks, labels and shipping papers must agree with the actual prepared shipment; placing an extra label on a wrong package does not cure the package defect.

Worked case: the paperwork names one product, the drum label another and the filling record a third batch. Stop the shipment and trace the actual contents and preparation records. Do not make the documents agree by simply copying the most convenient name. Once the authorized decision is made, correct all affected records and packaging elements and preserve the discrepancy and resolution. The receiving carrier's acceptance is not proof that the shipper's classification and packaging responsibilities were met.`,[
 ['A UN package marking authorizes what?','Only uses meeting the applicable packaging and material conditions.','Every chemical.','Any quantity by any mode.','Any closure method.','Package authorization depends on the actual material and configuration.'],
 ['Paper, label and filling record disagree. What next?','Hold and establish actual contents before coordinated correction.','Copy the label into every field.','Ship and let the carrier decide.','Remove the conflicting document.','Consistency must reflect truth, not cosmetic matching.'],
 ['Does carrier acceptance prove shipper compliance?','No.','Yes automatically.','Yes for a familiar carrier.','Yes after a phone call.','The shipper retains its applicable responsibilities.']]],
 ['Load, communicate emergencies and protect shipment security',`Loading decisions include package condition, securement, orientation, segregation and compatibility under the applicable mode and material rules. A package with leakage or damage needs the appropriate controlled response, not an extra wrap and quick dispatch. Protect packages from movement and damage while keeping required markings and information usable. Drivers and loaders must understand who verifies each part of the handoff.

Emergency response information and the required contact arrangements must correspond to the shipment and remain available as required. A phone number that nobody can answer appropriately does not create a response capability. Workers should know how to report a leak, isolate themselves and others from exposure and summon trained help. They should not open an unknown drum to identify the contents during an incident.

Security awareness includes recognizing suspicious access, unexplained changes and unusual requests for shipment information. Where a security plan is required, affected employees need the applicable in-depth instruction and their assigned duties. Do not publish sensitive route or security details in a general course record.

Worked case: someone requests a last-minute delivery address change through an unverified message. Pause the change and use the established verification channel. Record the verified operational instruction without exposing the security plan. Schedule pressure does not justify sending hazardous material to an unconfirmed destination or leaving it unsecured while staff investigate.`,[
 ['A drum leaks at loading. What next?','Hold shipment and use the assigned safe response procedure.','Wrap it and dispatch.','Open it to smell the contents.','Hide it behind intact drums.','Leakage requires controlled action and appropriate personnel.'],
 ['What must emergency information match?','The actual shipment and required response arrangements.','Any similar chemical.','Only the carrier’s logo.','Only a generic office number.','Responders need accurate material-specific information and usable contacts.'],
 ['An unverified message changes the destination. What next?','Verify through the established authorized channel.','Send it immediately.','Post the route publicly for advice.','Ignore security procedures for urgent loads.','Security controls protect against unauthorized shipment diversion.']]],
 ['Test the actual job and certify its training scope',`Build an assessment around the employee's real function. A packer demonstrates the authorized package, condition checks and closure instructions using safe training materials. A shipping-paper preparer reconciles identity and required entries. A loader demonstrates the applicable securement and compatibility decisions. A driver addresses the additional requirements of the assigned mode and vehicle duties. Do not replace all these functions with a single general awareness quiz.

Use a deliberately inconsistent training shipment to test whether the person stops and resolves discrepancies. Include one damaged package, one wrong closure instruction or one missing required information element. The evaluator must know the applicable requirements and the function being assessed. A dangerous real shipment must not be used as an uncontrolled experiment.

The federal recurrent training interval is at least once every three years under the HMR, with other applicable modal requirements considered. Maintain the employee's name, most recent completion date, training description or materials location, trainer name and address and the employer's certification that training and testing were completed, as required. Retain current records covering the preceding three years during employment and for ninety days afterward. A failed classification or packaging decision needs targeted instruction and retesting before independent assignment. This VNDRLY module contributes common knowledge; the employer must document the additional function-specific content and results rather than labeling general completion as qualification for every commodity and mode.`,[
 ['What should a packer’s assessment include?','The actual authorized package and closure method.','Only hazard-symbol recognition.','Only driving history.','Only course attendance.','Function-specific competence must address the work performed.'],
 ['Federal HMR recurrent training is required at least how often?','Every three years.','Every ten years.','Only after an accident.','Never after initial training.','Applicable modal requirements may be more restrictive.'],
 ['Who certifies the employee received the required training and testing?','The employer under the applicable record requirements.','The carrier by accepting freight.','The course title automatically.','Any customer who sees a badge.','Employer certification must be supported by actual training and testing.']]]
]);

program('OG37',[osha('1910.1030')],
 ['Exposure determination','Clean-to-contaminated workflow','Immediate exposure response','Interactive and practical assessment'],
 ['Actual exposure-control plan, interactive questions with a knowledgeable person, vaccination offer and confidential clinical follow-up remain employer responsibilities.'],[
 ['Determine occupational exposure from duties, not reassurance',`Bloodborne-pathogen protection applies where occupational exposure is reasonably anticipated from assigned duties. Exposure determination considers the tasks without relying on PPE to erase the exposure category. First aid, handling contaminated items and some cleanup tasks can create contact with blood or other potentially infectious materials. The employer's exposure-control plan identifies affected work, controls and response arrangements.

Universal precautions treat human blood and specified body fluids as potentially infectious. A person's appearance, reputation or willingness to say they are healthy is not a control. Exposure routes include a contaminated sharp penetrating skin, contact with nonintact skin and splashes to mucous membranes. Intact skin and a needlestick are not identical situations, but workers should report potential exposure promptly through the plan rather than self-diagnose risk and delay.

Worked case: a field first aider helps a coworker with a bleeding hand. The responder uses the assigned barriers and safe technique and keeps clean equipment separated from contaminated items. Afterward, the coworker says no infection is possible. That reassurance does not justify unsafe cleanup or cancel the required response to a real exposure. The worker's role and the actual contact determine the next steps. The employer provides appropriate information and controls in language the employee understands and makes the plan accessible; an online course is not a substitute for those site arrangements.`,[
 ['Does a person’s healthy appearance eliminate bloodborne risk?','No.','Yes if they are a coworker.','Yes if they deny infection.','Yes if the injury is small.','Universal precautions do not depend on personal reassurance.'],
 ['Is exposure determination made by assuming PPE removes the exposure category?','No.','Yes with gloves.','Yes with goggles.','Yes with an apron.','The determination considers assigned exposure tasks without crediting PPE as elimination.'],
 ['What is a relevant exposure route?','A splash to a mucous membrane.','Reading a first-aid poster.','Standing in a clean office.','Handling sealed clean supplies.','Mucous-membrane contact can be an occupational exposure.']]],
 ['Arrange cleanup so clean hands stay clean',`Engineering and work-practice controls reduce contact before PPE is relied on. Use suitable sharps containers and handling tools, organize clean supplies away from contaminated work and avoid practices that splash or aerosolize material. Do not pick up contaminated broken glass directly with hands. Use the designated mechanical means and disposal system. Do not recap or manipulate contaminated needles outside the narrow permitted circumstances and approved method.

Choose PPE for the expected contact: gloves, protective clothing and face or eye protection as the task requires. Inspect it and replace damaged items. Gloves do not make everything touched clean. Remove them using the taught method, avoid touching clean surfaces with contaminated gloves and wash hands as required after removal. Where immediate handwashing facilities are not feasible, use the provided interim method and wash with soap and running water as soon as feasible.

Worked case: a responder uses a contaminated glove to open the clean supply cabinet and answer a shared phone. The exposure pathway has spread. Stop the clean-to-dirty crossover, arrange appropriate decontamination and reorganize supplies and communication. Use the employer-approved disinfectant, concentration and contact time for the surface; do not mix cleaners or assume a quick dry wipe is equivalent. Handle laundry and regulated waste through their assigned containers and labeling systems without sorting or compressing contaminated items by hand.`,[
 ['How should contaminated broken glass be collected?','With the designated mechanical means, not directly by hand.','With bare fingers.','With gloved fingers as the default.','By pressing it into a bag.','Mechanical handling reduces sharps injury risk.'],
 ['Do contaminated gloves make a shared phone safe to touch?','No.','Yes if gloves are intact.','Yes for a quick call.','Yes if the phone is plastic.','Gloves can transfer contamination to clean surfaces.'],
 ['What determines disinfectant use?','The approved product instructions, concentration and contact time.','A quick wipe regardless of product.','Mixing cleaners for strength.','The strongest smell.','Effective decontamination depends on correct application.']]],
 ['Act immediately after exposure and protect confidentiality',`After a needlestick or other potential exposure, perform the appropriate immediate washing or flushing, report promptly and obtain the employer-arranged confidential medical evaluation and follow-up. Do not use caustic chemicals, deliberately squeeze a wound as a treatment or delay while deciding whether the source person seems healthy. Clinical professionals determine appropriate testing, prophylaxis and follow-up; a supervisor or course score does not make that decision.

Provide accurate information about the route, circumstances, time and material involved. Source-person testing and disclosure follow the applicable requirements and consent rules; workers must not conduct their own informal medical investigation. Medical details belong in protected clinical records, while the operational review can address the task and failed controls without broadcasting diagnoses.

The covered employer offers hepatitis B vaccination to eligible employees under the standard's conditions, generally within ten working days of initial assignment after required training, unless a specified exception applies. A worker who initially declines may later accept under the applicable provisions. Declining vaccination does not waive safe work practices or exposure follow-up.

Worked case: a responder notices blood in a splash to the eye after assisting a worker. They begin the appropriate flushing and report immediately for evaluation. The team does not wait until the shift ends to complete a form first. Documenting promptly supports care, but paperwork must not obstruct urgent action.`,[
 ['A blood splash reaches an eye. What is the immediate priority?','Appropriate flushing, prompt reporting and medical evaluation.','Finish the shift before reporting.','Ask coworkers to diagnose infection.','Apply a harsh disinfectant to the eye.','Immediate action and qualified follow-up should not be delayed.'],
 ['Who determines exposure medical follow-up?','The qualified healthcare professional through the employer arrangement.','The course platform.','A supervisor from a quiz score.','The exposed worker’s coworkers.','Clinical decisions require appropriate medical expertise and confidentiality.'],
 ['Does declining hepatitis B vaccination waive other protections?','No.','Yes including follow-up.','Yes for one year.','Yes if a form is signed.','Vaccination decisions do not remove exposure-control duties.']]],
 ['Combine interactive questions with observed safe handling',`Covered training must allow interactive questions and answers with a person knowledgeable in the subject as it relates to the workplace. A static lesson and automated feedback alone do not establish that requirement. Provide a real opportunity to discuss the actual exposure-control plan, tasks, reporting arrangements and controls in an understandable format. Record the session and unresolved questions accurately.

Use clean training materials and a simulated contamination marker to observe PPE selection, clean and contaminated zones, sharps-container decisions, glove removal, hand hygiene and the immediate exposure-reporting sequence. Do not use real blood or contaminated sharps to make the exercise realistic. The evaluator should identify whether the learner transfers simulated contamination to the face, clean supplies or shared equipment and teach how to correct the workflow.

Passing requires recognition of exposure routes, correct barrier use, safe disposal, prompt reporting and confidentiality. Hand collection of simulated sharps, ignoring a splash or cleaning a training wound with a caustic chemical is a critical failure. Stop and provide targeted practice before reassessment. Initial and annual training and additional instruction for changed exposure tasks must follow the standard. Keep medical and vaccination records separate from routine learning results. This online course can record knowledge completion, while the interactive session and actual practical observations remain honestly identified as separate evidence.`,[
 ['Can automated feedback alone prove the required interactive Q&A occurred?','No.','Yes if lengthy.','Yes if all answers are correct.','Yes if available overnight.','The rule requires an opportunity with a knowledgeable person.'],
 ['What materials are appropriate for a handling drill?','Clean training items and simulated contamination.','Real contaminated sharps.','Unlabeled biological waste.','Actual blood on shared tools.','Skills can be demonstrated without introducing infectious exposure.'],
 ['Where do confidential clinical details belong?','In the protected medical system, separate from routine course records.','In a public completion badge.','In a team chat.','On the cleanup checklist visible to everyone.','Operational learning does not require broadcasting private medical information.']]]
]);

program('OG38',[osha('1910.157'),osha('1910.156')],
 ['Evacuation versus response assignment','Fire classes and extinguisher limits','Incipient-stage decisions','Separate extinguisher and brigade assessment'],
 ['Actual response policy, equipment practice and brigade organization/training remain required. No interior firefighting qualification or unverified NFPA edition claim is issued.'],[
 ['Know whether the employer expects evacuation or response',`The first decision is the employer's emergency policy. Some workers evacuate when fire is discovered; others are designated to use portable extinguishers within a defined scope. An organized fire brigade has additional duties, organization, training and equipment requirements. A worker should not infer a firefighting assignment because an extinguisher hangs nearby or because this course mentions its operation.

Know how to raise the alarm, contact emergency services, evacuate and account for people. A response attempt must not delay warning or trap others. The route should remain available from the actual work position, and workers should know the alternatives if smoke or fire blocks it. Do not use a lift or enter a smoke-filled corridor merely because it is the familiar way out; follow the building and site emergency plan.

Worked case: a contractor sees a small flame beside a process skid but has been briefed to evacuate only. Their correct action is alarm and safe evacuation with useful information for responders, not an extinguisher attempt to prove initiative. Another site may assign trained personnel to incipient-stage response. The difference is the established role and readiness, not personal confidence. Course completion is knowledge evidence and must not silently change the employer's written response organization or expand a worker's authority during an emergency.`,[
 ['An evacuation-only worker sees a small fire. What should they do?','Raise the alarm and follow safe evacuation instructions.','Assume an extinguisher assignment.','Enter smoke to investigate.','Wait for flames to grow.','The employer’s response policy defines the worker’s role.'],
 ['Does an extinguisher on the wall assign every worker to firefight?','No.','Yes automatically.','Yes after this quiz.','Yes if the worker is nearby.','Equipment availability is not role authorization.'],
 ['What must a response attempt preserve?','Alarm, escape and protection of others.','Production continuity at any cost.','Secrecy until the fire is out.','The worker’s original route even if blocked.','A response must not create entrapment or delay emergency activation.']]],
 ['Match the extinguisher to the material and its limits',`Fire classes identify different burning materials and electrical conditions. Ordinary combustible solids, flammable liquids, energized electrical equipment, combustible metals and cooking media can require different extinguishing agents. Read the actual extinguisher label and site instructions. A familiar red cylinder is not proof of suitability for every fire. Water on an unsuitable electrical or reactive-material fire can worsen the hazard.

An extinguisher has limited capacity and discharge duration. Its range, agent and operating method depend on the model. Inspect accessibility, visible condition and the required readiness indicators through the site program; an inspection tag does not make an empty or damaged unit usable. Keep access clear, and report missing, discharged or defective equipment for proper service. Do not return a partially discharged unit to the wall as ready.

Worked case: a worker approaches a burning liquid spill with an extinguisher marked only for ordinary combustibles. The correct decision is to maintain safe distance, activate the plan and obtain the designated response rather than try the wrong agent. Even a correctly rated extinguisher does not make a large, spreading or smoke-obscured fire suitable for an incipient-stage attempt. The worker must know when the environment or the fuel source exceeds the equipment and their assigned capability before attempting discharge.`,[
 ['Does cylinder color establish extinguisher suitability?','No; read its actual rating and instructions.','Yes for every red unit.','Yes if new.','Yes if wall mounted.','Agent and rating, not color alone, determine application.'],
 ['A partially discharged extinguisher should be treated how?','Removed from ready status for appropriate service or replacement.','Returned as fully ready.','Hidden behind another unit.','Marked ready if the pin is replaced.','Remaining charge cannot be assumed adequate.'],
 ['A correct fire-class rating guarantees a safe attempt. Correct?','No; size, smoke, escape and role limits still apply.','Yes universally.','Yes if two workers are present.','Yes until the cylinder empties.','Equipment suitability is only one part of the decision.']]],
 ['Use a conservative incipient-stage decision',`Incipient-stage firefighting is limited to the beginning stage that can be controlled with portable extinguishers or other specified small systems without the need for protective clothing or breathing apparatus. Smoke, heat, growth, hidden fuel or an uncertain atmosphere can put the event beyond that scope. A worker should not enter a hazardous environment to determine whether the fire is still small enough.

For an authorized trained user, keep a safe escape behind you, approach only within the assigned method and use the actual extinguisher instructions. Common pull-aim-squeeze-sweep language is a memory aid, not a substitute for hands-on equipment practice, agent selection and the decision to withdraw. Stop the attempt if conditions worsen, visibility decreases, escape is threatened or the fire is not promptly controlled. Do not keep fighting until the last agent is gone if withdrawal is already indicated.

Worked case: a small bin fire begins to involve an adjacent wall and produces increasing smoke. The original small-fire decision is no longer valid. Withdraw, maintain the alarm and provide responders with the location and materials involved. Do not reenter after the flame disappears; residual heat and atmosphere may remain hazardous. Reentry and restoration follow the responsible emergency authority's assessment. Preserve accountability at muster so an unnecessary search is not launched for someone who left without reporting.`,[
 ['Smoke increases and fire spreads beyond the original bin. What next?','Withdraw and maintain emergency response.','Continue until all agent is gone.','Enter deeper to find the source.','Close the exit behind you.','The event has exceeded the initial response conditions.'],
 ['What does PASS-style wording replace?','Neither practical training nor the decision to withdraw.','All equipment instruction.','All fire-class selection.','All escape planning.','A mnemonic cannot establish competent equipment use or situational judgment.'],
 ['Flames disappear. Who authorizes reentry?','The responsible emergency authority after assessment.','Any worker who sees no flame.','The course administrator.','The person carrying the empty extinguisher.','Residual hazards can persist after visible flame ends.']]],
 ['Evaluate extinguisher users separately from brigade members',`For designated extinguisher users, use a suitable simulator or professionally controlled exercise to demonstrate identification, readiness, alarm, escape position, operation and withdrawal. The evaluator should include a wrong-agent example and a changing-fire scenario. Never create an uncontrolled process-area fire as a training exercise. General employee education and designated-user training have applicable initial and annual requirements under the portable-extinguisher rule.

A fire brigade needs a written organizational statement addressing its structure, functions and training, with appropriate equipment and personnel readiness. Training must fit the duties. Under the cited rule, brigade members receive training at least annually, and members expected to perform interior structural firefighting receive it at least quarterly. Other applicable requirements may be more demanding. A generic extinguisher demonstration does not establish interior structural, hazardous-material or technical-rescue competence.

The extinguisher rubric requires correct role decision, suitable agent, maintained escape and timely withdrawal. Entering smoke beyond the assignment, using an incompatible agent or ignoring a threatened exit is a critical failure. Record the actual equipment and observed outcome, and remediate before reassessment. Brigade evaluation requires its own duty-specific drills, leadership and records. Keep the VNDRLY knowledge certificate, the practical extinguisher result and brigade qualifications separate so the emergency roster contains only capabilities actually established and maintained.`,[
 ['Does an extinguisher drill establish interior structural firefighting competence?','No.','Yes with a high score.','Yes if the fire was real.','Yes for all experienced workers.','Brigade duties require a separate training and readiness system.'],
 ['Under the cited rule, expected interior structural brigade members train at least?','Quarterly.','Once in a career.','Every five years.','Only after a callout.','The fire-brigade rule specifies a distinct frequency for those duties.'],
 ['Which behavior is a critical extinguisher-evaluation failure?','Continuing while the escape route is threatened.','Raising the alarm.','Rejecting an unsuitable agent.','Withdrawing from increasing smoke.','Maintaining escape is essential to bounded incipient-stage response.']]]
]);
program('OG31',[osha('1926.451'),osha('1926.454')],
 ['User versus erector roles','Capacity foundations and access','Inspection and changed conditions','Role-specific scaffold demonstration'],
 ['Actual scaffold design, competent-person inspection, erection supervision and observed work remain external to online completion.'],[
 ['Different scaffold duties require different instruction',`A scaffold user needs to recognize electrical, fall and falling-object hazards, understand correct use and know the maximum intended load and load-carrying capacities. Under the construction rule, the user receives training from a person qualified in the subject. Erecting, dismantling, moving, operating, repairing, maintaining or inspecting a scaffold requires the specified training by a competent person appropriate to those activities. A user course does not make its graduate an erector or inspector.

Scaffold erection, movement, dismantling or alteration must occur under the applicable competent-person direction and supervision, with the required experienced and trained employees. A worker who removes a brace to make room for a valve changes the structure even if the task is called maintenance. Do not alter ties, braces, platforms or guardrails without the proper process. A tag may communicate inspection status but is not a substitute for the structure being safe now.

Worked case: a painter finds that a scaffold platform does not reach the work. Standing on a loose box or moving a plank without authorization creates another fall and stability problem. Stop and obtain an approved access arrangement. The appropriate person reviews the changed reach, loads and protection. Explain the defect to the supervisor with the scaffold location and intended task so the repair addresses the actual need rather than merely replacing the color of the tag.`,[
 ['Does scaffold user training authorize removing braces?','No; alteration requires the appropriate trained and supervised role.','Yes for access.','Yes if temporary.','Yes if the tag is green.','Removing a structural component is an alteration, not ordinary use.'],
 ['A platform cannot reach the task. What next?','Obtain an approved access arrangement.','Stand on a loose box.','Shift a plank without authorization.','Climb the guardrail.','Improvised height increases fall and stability risks.'],
 ['A scaffold tag establishes what?','Communicated status that still depends on actual safe conditions.','Permanent safety despite later changes.','Unlimited load capacity.','Permission for any user to alter it.','Tags do not override present defects or role limits.']]],
 ['Read loads, foundations and access as one system',`The intended load includes people, tools, stored materials and forces transmitted through the scaffold. Do not treat a load rating as a per-person allowance while ignoring stacked equipment. Components and foundations must support the applicable loads, and the scaffold must be built to the design and manufacturer's requirements. Mixing incompatible parts or placing legs on improvised unstable supports can defeat the whole system.

Access must be suitable for the scaffold and maintained clear. Cross braces are not a general substitute for proper access. Platforms need the required decking, spacing and protection; missing boards or excessive gaps cannot be made acceptable with caution tape alone. Guardrails and personal fall protection depend on the scaffold type and applicable requirements. Electrical clearance must be evaluated for the actual line voltage and work conditions; a familiar-looking cable is not evidence that it is deenergized.

Worked case: a crew adds several heavy valve assemblies to a platform originally planned for two workers and hand tools. Pause and obtain a load review before storage or work continues. The scaffold's footprint does not reveal its capacity. Consider concentrated loads, intended work positions and the foundation beneath. If a base settles, do not level the platform by wedging scrap material under it while workers remain above. Remove exposure and have the authorized team correct the condition through the proper method.`,[
 ['Which items count toward intended scaffold loading?','People, tools, materials and relevant transmitted forces.','Only people.','Only stored steel.','Only items heavier than one worker.','Capacity decisions must include the complete intended load.'],
 ['Can cross braces normally replace proper access?','No.','Yes if climbed slowly.','Yes for experienced users.','Yes while carrying tools.','Access must use the suitable provided system.'],
 ['A base settles under an occupied scaffold. What next?','Remove exposure and obtain authorized correction.','Add scrap wedges while occupied.','Move the load to the edge.','Change the tag date.','Foundation movement can compromise the whole structure.']]],
 ['Inspect after changes, not only after erection',`A competent person inspects scaffolds and components for visible defects before each work shift and after occurrences that could affect structural integrity. Weather, impact, unauthorized alteration, foundation movement and changed loading can make the original condition obsolete. Users should report defects and stay off unsafe structures; they need not personally redesign the scaffold to exercise stop-work judgment.

Consider wind, storms, slippery platforms and debris under the applicable rules and competent-person decisions. Falling-object protection may require toe boards, screens, debris nets, canopies or barricaded areas as appropriate. A hard hat does not remove the need to prevent objects falling onto people. Plan tool and material handling so access is not blocked and the platform is not overloaded.

Worked case: a forklift bumps a scaffold leg after the morning inspection. The operator sees no obvious bend and suggests continuing. Stop use in the affected area and obtain competent inspection and correction before release. Preserve the event information so the inspector understands what changed. Do not let the morning tag hide the impact. Similarly, a removed tie must be addressed as a structural change, not a paperwork discrepancy. The handback should identify the inspected arrangement, restrictions and any repair, and it should reach the people who will use the scaffold next.`,[
 ['A forklift strikes a scaffold after inspection. What next?','Stop use and obtain the required inspection and correction.','Rely on the morning tag.','Continue if no bend is visible.','Cover the impact mark.','Impact can affect structural integrity without an obvious external sign.'],
 ['When does the construction rule require competent inspection?','Before each work shift and after integrity-affecting occurrences.','Only once after erection.','Only after an injury.','Only when a tag is lost.','Inspection must track both routine use and relevant events.'],
 ['Does a hard hat replace falling-object controls?','No.','Yes for small tools.','Yes below a tagged scaffold.','Yes during short tasks.','Prevention and area protection remain necessary.']]],
 ['Assess users and erectors against different rubrics',`For a user assessment, use a safe scaffold or detailed training model. Ask the learner to identify access, platform completeness, fall protection, load limits, electrical hazards and the area below. Give a task with an added material load or missing guardrail and require a correct refusal and report. The learner should demonstrate safe access and handling only on a controlled, verified arrangement.

For an erector or alteration role, the evaluator must have the appropriate competence and assess the actual scaffold type, design instructions, foundation, bracing, ties, platform installation, protection and dismantling sequence. This is separate from the user exercise. Erection may itself involve fall exposure, so the training plan must provide the applicable protections and competent direction. Do not create an unstable scaffold to test whether a learner notices it while climbing.

Passing requires correct role limits and observable performance of the assigned tasks. Unauthorized alteration, climbing an unsafe structure, ignoring electrical clearance or accepting an unknown load limit is a critical failure. Stop, instruct and reassess. Record scaffold type, role, evaluator, observations and restrictions. Retraining is required when changes or observed inadequacies make it necessary. An online pass supports knowledge but does not document erection skill, a professional design or the daily competent-person inspection of a real scaffold. Keep those records distinct and tied to the actual location and date.`,[
 ['What should a user do with a missing guardrail?','Stop affected use and report for proper correction.','Remove another rail to match.','Work faster.','Assume the tag permits it.','Users must not rely on an incomplete protective arrangement.'],
 ['What must an erector evaluation address beyond user awareness?','Actual scaffold-type erection and alteration performance.','Only the course title.','Only attendance time.','Only a label quiz.','The role involves structural tasks requiring demonstrated skill.'],
 ['Can online completion stand in for today’s scaffold inspection?','No.','Yes for thirty days.','Yes for any small scaffold.','Yes if the learner is a supervisor.','A site inspection evaluates a real current structure.']]]
]);

program('OG32',[osha('1926.1428'),osha('1926.1419'),osha('1926.1422'),'https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1926/1926SubpartCCAppA'],
 ['Signal system and role','Dynamics and visibility','Emergency stop and handover','Written/oral plus practical evaluation'],
 ['Practical signaling evaluation and employer or third-party qualification documentation remain required; full standard hand-signal chart must be taught and demonstrated in the actual assessment.'],[
 ['Use an agreed signaling language',`A signal person is needed in covered crane operations when the point of operation is not fully visible, travel vision is obstructed or site-specific safety concerns make signaling necessary. The person must know and understand the signals used, be competent in applying them and understand the relevant equipment limitations and requirements. The crane operator must understand the same communication system.

Use the standard hand-signal method where required. A hand-signal chart must be posted on the equipment or conspicuously near the hoisting operation. A written lesson can teach the rule but cannot prove that a worker can produce and recognize the full set of gestures. Study the actual chart and practice under the evaluator. Nonstandard signals are limited to the circumstances allowed by the rule and must be agreed before use; improvising a gesture during a suspended load is not acceptable.

Read the linked OSHA Appendix A illustrations while learning these distinctions. Hoist uses the upward-pointing forearm and index finger making small circles; lower points the arm and index finger downward with small circles. Boom up and boom down use a horizontally extended arm with the thumb upward or downward and the other fingers closed. Swing points the extended index finger toward the intended boom movement. Stop uses one horizontally extended arm, palm down, moving back and forth; emergency stop uses both arms in that pattern. Dog everything brings the hands together at waist level. Extend a telescoping boom with both hands near the waist and thumbs outward; retract with thumbs toward each other. Move slowly adds a hand in front of the action-signaling hand. Main-hoist selection starts by tapping the top of the head; auxiliary-hoist selection taps the elbow of the vertically held forearm, followed by the action signal. The chart also teaches combined boom/load motion, travel, crawler-track and trolley signals; learn the applicable complete set from its illustrations rather than improvising from this text.

Worked case: a new subcontractor uses a familiar gesture that the operator interprets differently. Stop and establish the common method before movement. For radio or voice signals, agree the channel, identification, direction terminology and message structure. Test the system at the actual positions and noise conditions. Directional instructions must be clear from the agreed perspective, and the signal person must know which movement is commanded rather than merely repeating words heard from another worker.`,[
 ['Operator and signal person interpret a gesture differently. What next?','Stop and establish the common signal method before moving.','Let the operator guess.','Repeat it faster.','Use louder gestures.','Ambiguous communication cannot control a lift safely.'],
['Which distinction separates standard stop from emergency stop?','Stop uses one horizontally extended arm; emergency stop uses both.','Stop points a thumb upward; emergency stop downward.','Stop circles one finger; emergency stop taps the head.','There is no distinct emergency-stop signal.','The Appendix A stop patterns distinguish one arm from both arms, with palms down and back-and-forth motion.'],
 ['Can gestures be improvised during the lift?','Not outside the rule’s agreed nonstandard-signal conditions.','Yes whenever convenient.','Yes if both workers are experienced.','Yes for light loads.','Nonstandard signals require prior agreement and permitted circumstances.']]],
 ['Anticipate crane motion and the load path',`A signal person must understand basic crane dynamics, including swing, stopping, boom deflection and effects that make a suspended load behave differently from a rigid cart. Starting and stopping can create motion after a command ends. A load can rotate or drift, and a long object can sweep a larger area than its hook position suggests. Signals should support controlled movement with clearance and exclusion maintained throughout the path.

Position yourself where you can observe the relevant movement and communicate without standing in a pinch point, under the load or inside the equipment's hazardous swing area. Being close enough to touch the load is not a requirement for effective signaling. If the complete path cannot be seen, establish a coordinated arrangement that keeps responsibility and communication clear. Do not accept blind movement based only on yesterday's empty route.

Worked case: a pipe spool rotates as it passes a column. The hook is clear, but the trailing end approaches the structure and nearby workers. Signal a stop before the clearance is lost, communicate the issue and allow the lift team to revise the movement or control method. Do not try to arrest the spool with your body. The signal person communicates movement within the approved lift plan; they do not replace the operator's capacity determination or the rigger's load-attachment responsibilities.`,[
 ['A hook clears a column but a long load’s tail approaches it. What next?','Signal a stop and address the full load path.','Continue because the hook is clear.','Push the load away by hand from the pinch point.','Increase speed.','Clearance applies to the entire moving load.'],
 ['Where should the signal person stand?','Where observation and communication are effective outside hazard zones.','Under the load.','Between the load and a wall.','Inside the crane’s swing pinch point.','Signal effectiveness must not depend on personal exposure.'],
 ['Does signal-person qualification establish crane capacity authority for all lifts?','No.','Yes automatically.','Yes for small cranes.','Yes with a radio.','Operator, rigger and signal duties remain distinct.']]],
 ['Stop on lost communication and control the handover',`Only one person normally gives direction signals to a crane at a time under the applicable rule, while anyone who observes a safety problem may give the stop or emergency-stop signal. The operator must obey those stop signals. Do not ignore a stop because it came from someone other than the designated signal person. Resume only after the reason is understood and safe communication and conditions are restored.

If communication is interrupted, the operator must safely stop operations requiring signals until communication is reestablished and a proper signal is given and understood. Silence is not a command to continue. Radios should be tested and managed to prevent interference, but a working battery alone does not establish reliable communication in every location.

Worked case: the load passes behind a vessel, and another signal person will take over. Arrange the transfer before movement. Confirm who has control, that the new person can observe and that the operator understands the change. Avoid two people simultaneously giving conflicting directional commands. If a channel fails during the transition, stop rather than completing the last remembered motion. The handover must preserve load awareness, destination, restrictions and emergency communication, not merely pass a radio from one hand to another. Practice the transfer without a live suspended load before using it in a difficult setting.`,[
 ['A non-designated worker signals emergency stop. What should the operator do?','Obey the stop signal.','Ignore it because of role hierarchy.','Wait for a second signal.','Finish the movement first.','Anyone observing a safety problem may give the required stop signal.'],
 ['Radio contact is lost during a blind lift. What next?','Safely stop until proper communication is restored.','Continue the last command.','Guess the destination.','Increase speed to regain sight.','Loss of required signals removes movement control.'],
 ['What is essential during signal-person handover?','An explicit, understood transfer of directional control.','Both people signaling at once.','Only handing over the radio.','A silent change of position.','The operator must know whose direction is controlling the movement.']]],
 ['Demonstrate every signaling type claimed',`The construction qualification route requires knowledge established through an oral or written test and a practical test. A qualified third-party evaluator or the employer's qualified evaluator can perform the applicable evaluation. Documentation must identify each signaling type for which the person meets the requirements and be available at the site. An employer evaluator's qualification assessment under this option is not portable for other employers to use in the same manner as a third-party evaluation.

Use a controlled crane simulator, training rig or safely managed actual equipment. Assess recognition and production of the complete applicable standard hand signals, voice or radio commands, crane dynamics, visibility, positioning, emergency stop and lost communication. The evaluator should introduce ambiguity and a planned handover without exposing people to a suspended-load hazard. A learner who can identify a drawing but cannot give a clear gesture has not demonstrated that signaling type.

Passing requires accurate, understandable commands and safe response to interruption. Conflicting directions, failure to stop, unsafe positioning or incorrect movement commands are critical failures. Stop, instruct and reevaluate before duties resume. Record evaluator qualification basis, person, date, signaling types and observations. Reassessment is necessary when performance indicates the person lacks required knowledge or ability. This course supplies knowledge instruction and checks; it cannot fill in a practical pass or transferable third-party credential that has not actually occurred.`,[
 ['What tests does the covered qualification require?','Oral or written knowledge testing and practical testing.','Only attendance.','Only a quiz.','Only years employed.','The rule requires demonstrated practical ability as well as knowledge.'],
 ['Is an employer-evaluator assessment automatically portable to another employer?','No.','Yes for every crane.','Yes if laminated.','Yes if the worker requests it.','The employer-evaluator option has an explicit portability limitation.'],
 ['What must the documentation identify?','Each signaling type for which the person qualifies.','Only the job title.','Only the radio model.','Only course length.','Qualification scope must match the methods actually evaluated.']]]
]);

program('OG33',[osha('1926.251'),osha('1926.1404')],
 ['Weight center of gravity and geometry','Rigging ratings and inspection','Load control and landing','Task-specific rigger evaluation'],
 ['Actual load data, manufacturer ratings and task-specific practical qualification are required. The generic OSHA3529 PDF returned403; live regulation pages were used instead.'],[
 ['Establish weight and center of gravity before choosing gear',`A rigger needs reliable load weight, center of gravity, attachment points and the planned lift geometry. A shipping weight may exclude liquid, accessories or material left inside. A lifting lug's appearance does not prove its rating or suitability for the intended direction. Obtain the appropriate manufacturer or engineered information and resolve discrepancies before rigging. An uncertain load is not made known by choosing the largest sling in the store.

Sling force increases as sling legs become flatter. For an ideal, symmetrical two-leg system supporting a 4,000-pound load with each leg at 60 degrees above horizontal, the static leg tension is approximately 4,000 divided by twice the sine of 60 degrees, or 2,309 pounds. At 30 degrees it becomes 4,000 pounds per leg. This illustration assumes equal sharing and omits dynamic and other design factors; it is a teaching calculation, not a lift approval.

Worked case: an asymmetrical skid has heavy equipment at one end. Do not divide the weight equally between sling legs without evidence. The center of gravity and attachment geometry change how the load is shared and whether it will tilt. Obtain a suitable rigging plan within the rigger's actual competence. Complex, unusual or uncertain loads may require additional qualified engineering input rather than extending a basic rigger qualification by confidence alone.`,[
 ['In the stated ideal example, each leg at30 degrees carries approximately?','4,000 pounds.','2,000 pounds.','1,000 pounds.','500 pounds.','At30 degrees, twice the sine is one, so leg tension equals the total load in this idealized geometry.'],
 ['Does an asymmetrical skid necessarily share load equally?','No.','Yes with two slings.','Yes if lifted slowly.','Yes if hooks match.','Center of gravity and geometry determine load distribution.'],
 ['Can unknown weight be resolved by using the largest sling?','No; establish reliable load information.','Yes automatically.','Yes for short lifts.','Yes with a spotter.','A large component does not establish the load or the complete system capacity.']]],
 ['Read the rating for the actual hitch and reject defects',`A sling's rating depends on material, size, hitch, angle and the manufacturer's conditions. A vertical rating cannot be copied into a choker or basket arrangement without the applicable rating information. Bending over small diameters, sharp edges, heat, chemicals and twisting can reduce suitability. Protect the sling from edges with the appropriate rated or approved method; a piece of scrap cloth is not automatically adequate protection.

Inspect rigging before use each shift and as necessary during use under the construction rule. Required identification must be legible, and defective equipment is removed from service. Examine the applicable criteria for wire rope, chain, synthetic slings, hooks, shackles and other components. Do not invent a universal allowable cut or broken-wire limit across different products. Use the actual standard and manufacturer criteria for the equipment.

Worked case: a synthetic sling has no readable identification, while a shackle pin has been replaced with a similar bolt. Neither should be accepted because the lift is light. Quarantine the suspect equipment and obtain correctly identified, compatible components. Follow shackle loading and assembly instructions; side loading and multiple sling eyes can change suitability. The rigging system is limited by its weakest relevant component and configuration. A high-capacity sling cannot compensate for an unverified lifting point or an improperly loaded connector.`,[
 ['A sling’s tag is unreadable. What next?','Remove it from use pending proper disposition.','Estimate capacity from color.','Use it for light loads.','Copy a neighboring sling’s tag.','Required identification is part of safe capacity selection.'],
 ['Can a similar bolt replace a shackle pin?','Not as an improvised substitution.','Yes if it fits.','Yes if tightened hard.','Yes for a short lift.','Connector components must match the approved assembly.'],
 ['Does a vertical sling rating automatically apply to a choker?','No; use the actual hitch rating.','Yes in all cases.','Yes below ten feet.','Yes with a new sling.','Hitch and geometry affect rated capacity.']]],
 ['Control the suspended load without becoming the control',`Plan the travel path and landing before taking strain. Clear people from the fall zone as required, identify pinch points and prepare supports that will hold the load without trapping rigging or hands. A tag line may help control rotation where appropriate, but it must not entangle the handler or pull them into the load path. Never wrap it around the body. Do not use human strength as the primary answer to an unstable rigging arrangement.

A controlled initial lift can reveal tilt, unequal loading or snagging, but it is not permission to experiment with unknown capacity. Stop when the load does not behave as planned. Lower it to a safe supported position and correct the arrangement through the responsible lift team. Keep fingers out of the space between sling and load while tension is applied.

Worked case: a skid tilts toward an unexpected heavy component. The rigger signals stop and coordinates safe lowering; a coworker must not climb onto the light end to balance it. Reconcile the weight and center-of-gravity information and revise the plan. At landing, verify stable support before releasing tension and removing rigging. A load resting partly on debris can shift when the sling is pulled. Close the lift by inspecting gear and reporting any damage or abnormal loading observed.`,[
 ['A skid tilts unexpectedly. What next?','Stop, safely lower and correct the plan.','Have a worker stand on the light end.','Lift faster.','Pull harder on a tag line.','Unexpected behavior indicates the assumed load arrangement may be wrong.'],
 ['When may rigging be removed after landing?','After the load is stable on suitable support.','As soon as one corner touches.','While the load is still swinging.','Before support is checked.','Removing tension can expose an unstable landing condition.'],
 ['How should a tag line be handled?','Without wrapping it around the body or entering the hazard path.','Tied to the handler’s waist.','Wrapped around a wrist.','Used to support the load weight.','Entanglement can pull the handler into the moving load.']]],
 ['Qualify for the assigned rigging task',`Rigger qualification is task specific. A person may be competent for ordinary symmetrical loads yet lack the skills for unusual geometry, multiple cranes, critical lifts or specialty attachments. The employer must determine whether the person has the qualifications for the actual work where a qualified rigger is required. A generic card is evidence to examine, not proof of competence for every possible lift.

Use a safe training load and an approved training plan. The evaluator observes weight and center-of-gravity reasoning, inspection, rating interpretation, hitch selection, edge protection, connection, communication, exclusion and stable landing. Include a damaged sling, missing identification or changed load information as a desk or controlled setup problem. Do not actually load defective gear to see whether the learner reacts.

Passing requires correct component and configuration choices, safe body positioning and recognition of conditions outside the learner's scope. Unknown load acceptance, capacity misuse, entering beneath the load, improvised connectors or ignoring a tilted load are critical failures. Stop and remediate, then reassess a different case. Record the load type, gear, hitches, conditions, evaluator and restrictions. Keep crane-operation and signal-person qualifications separate. Where the actual task requires engineering or a lift director's decision, the rigger must obtain it rather than treating this course's formula example as design approval. The online result records knowledge only until observed performance is documented honestly.`,[
 ['A basic rigger card qualifies someone for every complex lift. Correct?','No; competence must match the actual task.','Yes if current.','Yes if the worker volunteers.','Yes if the load is valuable.','Qualification scope follows demonstrated knowledge and skills.'],
 ['Which is a critical failure?','Accepting an unknown load with guessed capacity.','Rejecting an unreadable sling.','Requesting load data.','Keeping out of the fall zone.','Unknown load and capacity assumptions undermine the entire lift.'],
 ['What should the practical record specify?','Load type, gear, hitches, observed performance and restrictions.','Only attendance.','Only shirt size.','An unlimited crane license.','A useful qualification record preserves actual task scope.']]]
]);

program('OG35',[osha('1910.332'),osha('1910.333')],
 ['Qualification and deenergization','Voltage verification logic','Energized-work limits and rescue','Equipment-specific demonstration'],
 ['No licensed NFPA70E text or adopted edition was verified. Employer electrical program, equipment hazard assessment and qualified-person practical evidence remain necessary.'],[
 ['Qualification is specific to the equipment and task',`Electrical work can expose a person to shock, arc flash, blast and unexpected movement. A qualified person has the relevant training and demonstrated familiarity with the construction and operation of the equipment and its hazards. Qualification for one task or voltage class does not automatically cover another. An unqualified worker can recognize damaged cords, maintain distance and report hazards without being authorized to open an energized enclosure.

The ordinary planning preference is to deenergize exposed live parts before work unless the applicable rule's exceptions are actually established. Production convenience alone is not an automatic justification for energized work. Identify all supplies, including backfeed, batteries, generators, control power and stored energy. A dark indicator or stopped motor does not prove electrical safety. The isolation, lockout/tagout and verification steps must follow the governing electrical safe-work requirements and employer program.

Worked case: a technician familiar with low-voltage controls is asked to troubleshoot a medium-voltage cabinet. The person must decline work outside their qualification and obtain the properly qualified resource. Their experience is relevant but not transferable by assumption. The task briefing should identify equipment, drawings, sources, boundaries, protection, test methods and emergency arrangements. If the drawing differs from the installed system, resolve the discrepancy before relying on it to establish the safe state.`,[
 ['Does qualification on one electrical task cover every voltage and device?','No.','Yes after one online course.','Yes for any licensed trade worker.','Yes if the panel is labeled.','Qualification is tied to relevant equipment, hazards and skills.'],
 ['A dark indicator proves absence of voltage. Correct?','No.','Yes if the motor stopped.','Yes after five minutes.','Yes if the switch is off.','Indicators and control states do not verify every energy source.'],
 ['A worker is assigned equipment outside their qualification. What next?','Obtain the appropriately qualified person and task plan.','Learn while energized.','Rely on thicker gloves alone.','Ask an unqualified coworker to watch.','Protection cannot replace missing task qualification.']]],
 ['Verify the electrical state with suitable instruments',`The qualified person verifies that equipment cannot restart and tests the circuit elements and parts to which workers will be exposed for absence of voltage under the applicable procedure. The test must address backfeed and induced or stored energy where relevant. A test at one convenient terminal cannot clear every conductor behind the enclosure. The instrument, probes and leads must be suitable for the voltage, measurement category and environment, and their condition must be checked.

Use the employer's approved instrument-verification method. A live-dead-live sequence establishes instrument operation on a known source, performs the absence test and rechecks operation afterward as the program requires. The OSHA rule expressly calls for checking test-equipment operation immediately before and after the test for circuits over 600 volts; employer procedures may apply this good practice more broadly. Do not treat an unreliable meter as proof of zero.

Worked case: a meter reads zero, but a damaged lead is discovered. The result is not trustworthy. Maintain the electrical precautions, obtain a suitable verified instrument and repeat the required verification through the qualified person. Do not touch the conductor to confirm the reading. Testing itself can be energized work, so the appropriate boundaries, protective equipment and work practices remain in place until the safe state is established.`,[
 ['A zero reading is obtained with a damaged lead. What follows?','The result is unreliable and must be properly reverified.','The conductor is proven safe.','The lead damage improves insulation.','A second touch confirms it.','A failed measurement path can produce false assurance.'],
 ['What must the test cover?','The relevant exposed circuit parts and possible energy sources.','Only the easiest terminal.','Only the indicator lamp.','Only the main switch label.','Verification must match the actual worker exposure.'],
 ['May a worker touch a conductor to verify zero?','No.','Yes with one finger.','Yes after a quiz.','Yes if the floor is dry.','Human contact is not a voltage test method.']]],
 ['Keep energized-work and emergency limits explicit',`If energized work is justified under the applicable requirements, it remains a controlled exception with qualified personnel, suitable protective measures and a task-specific plan. Shock protection and arc-flash protection address different hazards. A voltage-rated glove does not by itself establish adequate arc protection, and arc-rated clothing does not make direct contact safe. Boundaries, protective equipment and methods must come from the applicable employer assessment and adopted requirements, not guessed tables.

This course does not claim to reproduce NFPA70E or verify the site's adopted edition. The employer must identify the governing edition and actual equipment assessment where that standard is used. Do not import a boundary or PPE category from an unrelated panel. Equipment condition, task and available fault energy can affect the decision. Stop when labels, drawings or assessment data conflict rather than choosing the least restrictive number.

Worked case: a person contacts an energized conductor. Do not grab them and become another current path. Activate emergency response, have the appropriate source isolated safely and use only rescue methods for which the responder is trained and equipped. First aid follows scene safety and the responder's actual training. Keep others clear and preserve the equipment state for investigation after emergency needs are addressed. A bystander's online certificate does not authorize electrical rescue inside an energized boundary.`,[
 ['Does arc-rated clothing alone prevent electric shock?','No.','Yes at every voltage.','Yes if dry.','Yes with a hard hat.','Arc and shock protection address different mechanisms.'],
 ['Conflicting equipment assessments are found. What next?','Stop and obtain a qualified resolution.','Choose the smallest boundary.','Copy the neighboring panel’s label.','Ignore the conflict.','Protective decisions require a coherent equipment-specific basis.'],
 ['A person is in contact with an energized conductor. What should an unassigned bystander do?','Activate response and prevent further contact from safety.','Grab the person barehanded.','Enter with ordinary gloves.','Throw water on the equipment.','Unprotected contact can create another casualty.']]],
 ['Evaluate the actual electrical task under safe conditions',`The evaluator first identifies the qualification sought: equipment, voltage, task, tools and boundaries. Use deenergized training equipment or a properly controlled setting, with safe simulation where possible. The learner reads the work package, identifies supplies, explains the isolation and verification sequence and selects suitable test equipment. A practical examination must not expose an unqualified learner to an uncontrolled live cabinet.

Introduce an alternate supply, a drawing mismatch and a failed instrument check. The learner must stop at the appropriate point and obtain correction. Observe instrument handling, probe placement and the verification method under the qualified instructor's controlled plan. Separately assess recognition of damaged portable equipment, approach limits and emergency communication for workers whose duties do not include electrical testing.

Passing requires correct source identification, role boundaries, protective planning, suitable instruments and a defensible verified state. Bypassing an unknown source, relying on a defective meter, touching unverified parts or entering beyond qualification is a critical failure. Stop, teach and reassess before authorization. Record equipment and task scope, evaluator, date, observations and restrictions. Do not award an electrical license, universal qualified-person status or an NFPA credential from this course. The employer's adopted-edition review and actual hazard assessments remain separate documents; recording them as missing is more accurate than manufacturing a compliance statement from a passing score.`,[
 ['What should an electrical practical evaluation specify first?','The equipment, voltage and task scope being assessed.','An unlimited title.','Only lesson count.','Only time employed.','Qualification evidence must have a defined scope.'],
 ['A drawing omits an alternate supply. What is the correct response?','Stop and resolve the source discrepancy.','Proceed using the old drawing.','Ignore the supply if small.','Replace the meter battery and continue.','Unidentified energy invalidates the isolation basis.'],
 ['What can this course honestly record?','Knowledge completion and separately observed scoped performance.','An automatic electrical license.','Verified compliance with an unread NFPA edition.','Qualification for every electrical installation.','Records must reflect actual instruction and evidence.']]]
]);
program('OG27',[osha('1910.134')],
 ['Selection and unknown atmospheres','Medical fit and seal distinctions','Use care and failure response','Model-specific assessment'],
 ['Employer exposure assessment, medical determination, actual fit testing and observed respirator use are not supplied by an online course.'],[
 ['Choose protection from the exposure, not the shelf',`A respiratory protection decision begins with the contaminant, concentration, exposure pattern, oxygen availability and work conditions. Engineering controls come first where feasible; a respirator is not permission to leave an avoidable release uncontrolled. Air-purifying equipment removes specified contaminants within its approved limits. It does not supply oxygen. Supplied-air equipment has its own air-quality, pressure, duration, mobility and emergency limitations.

Worked case: a tank atmosphere is unknown and the worker finds an organic-vapor cartridge in the store. The cartridge label does not establish that the atmosphere is within its use conditions or that oxygen is adequate. Do not enter. The employer's respiratory program must make the appropriate selection and address the required immediately-dangerous-to-life-or-health arrangements where applicable. A disposable particulate respirator does not become a gas respirator by adding a second layer.

Selection includes the required protection factor, approved respirator configuration, cartridge or filter, change schedule and the worker's other PPE. Do not mix parts merely because threads fit. A cartridge change schedule must be based on appropriate information under the program; waiting until the wearer smells the contaminant is not a universally acceptable method. Explain the assigned limitations before the person relies on the equipment. Identify the program administrator who resolves an uncertain selection.`,[
 ['Can an air-purifying respirator supply missing oxygen?','No.','Yes with two cartridges.','Yes with a fresh filter.','Yes if worn tightly.','Air-purifying equipment does not provide an oxygen supply.'],
 ['The atmosphere is unknown. What follows?','Do not enter on an improvised cartridge selection.','Use the nearest organic-vapor cartridge.','Double a dust mask.','Rely on smell.','Unknown conditions require the program’s appropriate assessment and protection.'],
 ['Compatible respirator parts are selected how?','As an approved configuration under the manufacturer and program.','By thread fit alone.','By matching colors.','By lowest price.','Mixing components can invalidate the approved protective configuration.']]],
 ['Separate medical clearance, fit testing and seal checks',`Medical evaluation determines whether a person can use the assigned respirator under the relevant conditions. It precedes required fit testing and use. The qualified healthcare professional manages confidential medical information; the employer receives the permitted recommendation and limitations rather than placing the full questionnaire in a training record. A different respirator or changed conditions may require additional evaluation.

For tight-fitting respirators, fit testing evaluates the selected make, model, style and size on that person under the required protocol. It occurs before initial use, at least annually and when changes require it. A different facepiece is not covered merely because it has the same nominal size. A user seal check is performed each time the tight-fitting respirator is donned; it checks the immediate seal but cannot replace the fit test.

Worked case: a worker passed a fit test six months ago but now has facial hair crossing the seal. The old result does not make today's seal acceptable. Stop use of that tight-fitting arrangement and resolve the issue through the program. Also consider interference from eyewear, head protection and other equipment. The correct response is a suitable program decision, not overtightening straps until the facepiece hurts. Recheck the complete wearing arrangement before returning to exposure.`,[
 ['Which comes before required fit testing and use?','The required medical evaluation.','A production shift.','A completed exposure incident.','A coworker’s approval.','Medical suitability must be established before the required use sequence.'],
 ['A daily user seal check replaces annual fit testing. Correct?','No; they serve different purposes.','Yes if repeated three times.','Yes for new masks.','Yes if no odor is noticed.','Fit testing and seal checks are distinct requirements.'],
 ['Facial hair crosses the sealing surface after a fit test. What next?','Resolve the incompatible fit before using that arrangement.','Rely on the old pass.','Tighten indefinitely.','Cover the hair with tape.','A previous fit test does not establish the present seal.']]],
 ['Use, maintain and leave before protection fails',`Inspect the respirator before use under the program and manufacturer's instructions. Check the facepiece, valves, straps, connections, filters or cartridges and relevant air-supply components. Identify damage, missing parts, contamination and expiration or service-life concerns. Cleaning, disinfection, storage and maintenance must preserve the equipment's integrity and prevent another worker's contamination. Do not store a clean facepiece loose in a dusty toolbox or alter a valve to reduce breathing resistance.

During work, recognize the assigned signs of malfunction, leakage, breakthrough, increased breathing resistance or distress. Follow the program's exit procedure promptly. Do not remove the facepiece inside the contaminated area to adjust it or argue about whether the cartridge should have lasted longer. The problem is investigated from safety and corrected before return.

Worked case: a filter becomes difficult to breathe through during dusty maintenance. The worker leaves the exposure area using the established route, then reports the problem and obtains the appropriate replacement and assessment. A coworker's used filter is not a verified substitute. Emergency respirators require their specified inspection and readiness records; a sealed storage box does not prove that an air cylinder, battery or component remains ready indefinitely. Explain which checks are the user's duty and which require authorized servicing.`,[
 ['Breathing resistance rises during exposure. What next?','Leave through the planned safe procedure and address the fault.','Remove the facepiece in the dust.','Disable a valve.','Keep working until the filter tears.','Protection problems are resolved from a safe location.'],
 ['Where should a cleaned respirator be stored?','In the protected storage specified by the program.','Loose in a dusty toolbox.','On a contaminated bench.','Under a heavy load.','Storage must prevent contamination, damage and deformation.'],
 ['Who may improvise internal repairs?','No one outside the authorized maintenance arrangements.','Any wearer with tape.','Any coworker with spare parts.','Anyone who passed this quiz.','Respirator integrity depends on approved maintenance and components.']]],
 ['Observe use skills separately from online completion',`The evaluator uses the assigned respirator and a safe training setting. Confirm that medical and fit-test prerequisites are satisfied where required, without collecting confidential clinical details. Ask the learner to explain why this device was selected, its limitations and the relevant emergency actions. Then observe inspection, donning, adjustment, user seal check, doffing, cleaning and storage using the manufacturer's procedure.

Introduce a missing valve, unsuitable cartridge or simulated malfunction with training equipment. The learner must recognize the problem, reject the equipment or leave the simulated exposure and obtain correction. Do not expose the person to a toxic substance to prove that the respirator works. For supplied-air or emergency equipment, include the model-specific checks and escape arrangements that the actual assignment requires, under appropriately qualified instruction.

Passing requires correct equipment identification, readiness, handling, limitations and failure response. Wearing an incompatible configuration, ignoring a failed seal check or removing protection inside the simulated contaminated boundary is a critical failure. Stop and remediate before reassessment. Record the equipment, role, evaluator and observed results separately from the online score and fit-test record. Respirator training is at least annual under the covered rule, with additional retraining for changes, inadequate knowledge or other relevant conditions. A knowledge certificate cannot be labeled a medical clearance or fit-test result.`,[
 ['What must remain separate from the course score?','Medical determination and actual fit-test evidence.','The lesson title.','The learner’s chosen language.','The course date.','Different records establish different prerequisites.'],
 ['What is a critical practical failure?','Ignoring a failed user seal check.','Rejecting a missing valve.','Explaining limitations.','Cleaning by the approved method.','A failed seal check means the current protective fit cannot be relied on.'],
 ['How often is covered respirator training provided at minimum?','Annually, with additional retraining triggers.','Only once.','Every ten years.','Only after an injury.','The respiratory standard includes recurring and trigger-based training.']]]
]);

program('OG28',[osha('1910.146'),'https://www.osha.gov/publications/shib093013'],
 ['Measurement design','Instrument assurance','Sampling and interpretation','Gas-tester performance evaluation'],
 ['Actual detector manual, sensors, calibration gas, sampling train and permit criteria must be supplied. This does not issue OPITO AGT recognition.'],[
 ['Design the measurement around the hazard',`A gas test answers a defined question at a defined place and time. Start with the process chemicals, possible oxygen displacement or enrichment, flammable release and toxic contaminants. Select sensors and ranges that can detect the relevant hazards. A four-gas instrument measures its installed channels; it is not a universal chemical analyzer. An oxygen channel does not establish the absence of toxic gas, and a flammable channel does not necessarily measure every combustible vapor equally.

Define where the result is needed: the breathing zone, the interior of a vessel, connected spaces or a hot-work location. A reading at a doorway cannot automatically represent the entire space. Consider stratification, obstructions, dead spaces and sources that can change during the task. Sampling must not require an unprotected person to enter the hazard to obtain the reading.

Worked case: a cleaning product introduces a vapor not covered by the installed sensor suite. The tester must identify the measurement gap and obtain a suitable method and qualified interpretation. Writing zero in the permit because the detector has no alarm would manufacture assurance. The permit authority needs the real measurement limitations before authorizing work. Record the instrument identity and sensors so a later reviewer can understand what the readings could and could not show.`,[
 ['A detector lacks the relevant vapor sensor. What is a no-alarm result?','Insufficient evidence about that vapor.','Proof the vapor is absent.','A valid zero for the missing channel.','Automatic permit clearance.','A detector cannot measure a hazard outside its capability.'],
 ['A doorway reading represents every vessel pocket. Correct?','No; sampling locations must represent the actual hazards.','Yes if the doorway is large.','Yes after one minute.','Yes for any four-gas meter.','Atmospheric conditions can vary spatially.'],
 ['Where should sampling be performed from when possible?','A protected position using the approved method.','Inside the unknown atmosphere without protection.','At any convenient unrelated location.','Only in the office.','Obtaining a reading must not create an uncontrolled exposure.']]],
 ['Distinguish response checks from calibration',`A bump or functional check challenges the instrument with the specified gas to verify response and alarms. Calibration compares and adjusts measurement response as required by the manufacturer. Neither should be reduced to turning on the display. Follow the model's instructions for frequency, gas, concentrations, flow, adapters and acceptance criteria. A failed required check means the instrument cannot be relied on until corrected and released through the proper process.

Inspect the sampling path as well as the sensor. A blocked filter, leaking hose, failed pump or wrong regulator can invalidate remote sampling while the screen appears normal. Check calibration-gas identity, concentration, expiration and cylinder condition. Do not use a convenient cylinder with a different mixture and assume the result is equivalent. Fresh-air zeroing requires an atmosphere known to be appropriate; zeroing in contamination can hide the hazard.

Worked case: the tester replaces a hose with a much longer one and skips the pump check. The instrument's previous calibration does not prove the changed sampling assembly is functional. Verify the complete configuration, follow the required checks and account for the new transport time. Preserve failed checks and corrective action rather than overwriting the log with a later passing result. Record the changed hose length with the sampling setup.`,[
 ['What does a bump check primarily verify?','Response and alarm function to the specified challenge gas.','Permanent accuracy for all chemicals.','The entire worksite atmosphere.','The user’s medical fitness.','A response check has a defined instrument-assurance purpose.'],
 ['Can a calibrated sensor compensate for a leaking sampling hose?','No.','Yes automatically.','Yes if the battery is full.','Yes when the hose is new.','The entire measurement path must function.'],
 ['Where may fresh-air zeroing be performed?','In an atmosphere known to meet the required clean-air conditions.','Inside an unknown tank.','Beside the suspected leak.','Anywhere if the screen reads zero afterward.','Zeroing in contamination can create a false baseline.']]],
 ['Wait for a representative sample and interpret its limits',`Remote sampling includes gas travel through the line and the sensor's response time. Follow the manufacturer's method for the actual hose length, flow, sensor and gas. Do not record the first number displayed immediately after moving the probe. Purge and allow stabilization as required at each location. Some gases can interact with tubing or filters; use the approved materials and understand the limitations.

For a general-industry permit-space test, the standard specifies testing oxygen, then combustible gases and vapors, then toxic gases and vapors, using suitable calibrated equipment. Understand how the instrument performs that sequence and why oxygen conditions can affect some combustible-sensor interpretations. Percent of lower explosive limit is not percent oxygen and is not automatically a toxic exposure value. A low flammable reading can coexist with a hazardous toxic concentration.

Worked case: the permit requires specified acceptable conditions, but a new process stream begins during entry. Earlier readings do not authorize the changed state. Apply the required continuous monitoring or retesting, communicate changes and stop or evacuate when conditions fail. The tester reports values, units, time, location, equipment and limitations. The entry or hot-work authority makes the permit decision using that evidence and all other safeguards; the tester must not silently broaden a limited test into universal clearance.`,[
 ['A probe moves to a new location. When is a reading recorded?','After the required transport and response stabilization.','Immediately on movement.','Before the probe arrives.','Whenever the lowest number appears.','The sample must represent the new location.'],
 ['Does low percent LEL prove toxic exposure is acceptable?','No.','Yes for every chemical.','Yes if oxygen is normal.','Yes with a fresh battery.','Flammability and toxicity are different endpoints.'],
 ['A process stream changes during entry. What next?','Apply reassessment, monitoring and stop criteria for the changed conditions.','Keep using the original permit readings.','Delete the earlier test.','Turn off the alarm.','The original sampling condition no longer represents the whole task.']]],
 ['Evaluate an authorized tester on a controlled rig',`Use the actual instrument model and approved test apparatus in a safe area. The learner identifies the installed sensors, checks the gas and calibration status, assembles the sampling train and performs the required pre-use tests. Present a blocked line or deliberately incorrect training-gas label without exposing the learner to a dangerous atmosphere. They must reject the arrangement and explain the effect on evidence.

Next, provide a drawing of a vessel with several sampling points and a task description. The learner chooses locations, explains transport and response time and records simulated values with correct units. Include an oxygen problem and a toxic hazard outside the sensor suite. The correct response is to recognize limitations and withhold a false clearance, not to invent a reassuring number.

The rubric assesses selection, readiness, sampling technique, stabilization, interpretation, records and alarm response. Unsupported zero entries, skipped failed checks, unsafe entry to sample or permit clearance beyond the assigned authority are critical failures. Provide practice and reassess with different data. Record the model, sensors, sampling method, evaluator and task scope. Keep this authorization distinct from personal-monitor awareness. If a customer requires an OPITO AGT credential, use the approved product and assessment; this VNDRLY knowledge and employer evaluation record cannot impersonate that award.`,[
 ['Which practical error is critical?','Entering an unknown atmosphere unprotected to obtain a sample.','Rejecting the wrong test gas.','Recording units.','Acknowledging sensor limitations.','Testing must not create uncontrolled exposure.'],
 ['What distinguishes tester authorization from monitor awareness?','Demonstrated model-specific selection, sampling and interpretation skills.','The color of the badge.','A larger online score alone.','Owning a detector.','The testing role requires more than reacting to a personal alarm.'],
 ['A customer requires OPITO AGT. What does this course issue?','A VNDRLY learning record, not that external award.','An automatic OPITO credential.','A universal gas-testing license.','A rescue certification.','Named external credentials require their actual approved route.']]]
]);

program('OG29',[osha('1910.252'),osha('1910.157')],
 ['Permit area survey','Fire-watch decisions','Changes and post-work surveillance','Practical permit/watch assessment'],
 ['Actual hot-work permits and site watch periods remain necessary. No licensed NFPA edition was accessed or claimed as verified.'],[
 ['Survey where heat and sparks can travel',`Hot work includes activities that can create ignition, not only tasks called welding. Grinding, cutting and other heat-producing work can ignite materials beyond the immediate workpiece. First consider a cold method or relocation to an appropriate designated area. Where hot work remains necessary, identify combustibles, flammable atmospheres, connected equipment and openings that carry sparks or heat to another location.

A floor opening, cable penetration or metal wall can expose an unseen area. Remove or properly protect combustibles and assign coverage for the actual spread paths. The general-industry welding rule includes the familiar 35-foot combustible considerations, but distance alone does not make the rest of the building safe. Read the actual conditions and site permit; nearby combustible construction, concealed spaces and sparks traveling farther can require additional controls.

Worked case: a worker welds a bracket on one side of a steel partition while cardboard is stored on the other. A clear floor around the welder does not address conducted heat or openings. Inspect both sides and establish protection and watch arrangements before authorization. A signed permit cannot compensate for an inaccessible area that nobody has evaluated. Coordinate operations that could release flammables into the area during work, and define when testing and reauthorization are required.`,[
 ['Why inspect the other side of a metal partition?','Heat and sparks can ignite hidden adjacent materials.','Only to count workers.','Only to check paint color.','There is no reason if the front is clear.','Ignition pathways extend beyond the visible work face.'],
 ['Does a 35-foot survey automatically resolve every hazard?','No; actual spread paths and applicable conditions still matter.','Yes everywhere.','Yes for short jobs.','Yes if a permit is signed.','A distance rule does not eliminate hidden or extended ignition paths.'],
 ['What should be considered before authorizing hot work?','A suitable cold method or designated safe work area.','Only the worker’s speed.','Only the tool’s purchase date.','Only the weld length.','Avoiding ignition exposure can remove the need for complex controls.']]],
 ['The fire watch watches the exposure, not the schedule',`A fire watch must understand the hazards, alarm method and appropriate extinguishing equipment and have an unobstructed view or adequate coverage of the affected areas. They need authority to stop work. Assigning the title to someone who is also unloading a truck does not ensure the function is performed. If one person cannot observe the relevant locations, the plan needs additional coverage or a different work arrangement.

The watch distinguishes a small fire within assigned capability from an emergency requiring evacuation and trained response. Sound the alarm and summon help as the plan requires. Do not let an extinguisher attempt block escape or delay notification. Extinguisher familiarity does not qualify the watch for interior structural firefighting or entry into smoke.

Worked case: sparks fall through a grating to the level below while the watch remains beside the welder. The visible work point is not the whole exposure. Stop, establish lower-level protection and monitoring, and verify the permit arrangements before restarting. If the watch must leave, stop hot work or make a proper qualified handover. A phone left on the bench cannot observe a fire. The watch should know the exact stop signal and confirm the operator responds immediately. Test that communication before the first ignition-producing operation.`,[
 ['Sparks reach a lower level outside the watch’s view. What next?','Stop and establish adequate protection and coverage.','Continue because a watch is present.','Ask the welder to glance down occasionally.','Rely on smell.','Watch coverage must match the ignition exposure.'],
 ['The watch must leave. What is acceptable?','Stop work or transfer duties properly to a qualified replacement.','Leave the phone behind.','Ask the operator to watch while welding.','Return before anyone notices.','The function must remain effective throughout the required period.'],
 ['A developing fire exceeds assigned capability. What should the watch do?','Activate the emergency plan and withdraw safely.','Enter smoke to finish extinguishing.','Delay the alarm until the extinguisher is empty.','Use any available liquid.','Role limits and escape take priority over an uncontrolled firefighting attempt.']]],
 ['Changed conditions and post-work handback',`A permit applies to defined work and conditions. A nearby solvent transfer, ventilation failure, altered isolation or a break followed by changed surroundings can invalidate the original decision. Stop, communicate and obtain the required reassessment. Gas testing is only one part of this decision; a satisfactory reading does not eliminate combustible solids, hidden heat transfer or an incomplete isolation.

After hot work stops, heat can remain in material or concealed spaces. The general-industry welding rule requires a fire watch for at least half an hour after completion to detect and extinguish possible smoldering fires. The actual site or governing requirements may require longer watch or additional monitoring. Do not claim a universal NFPA duration or an adopted edition that has not been verified. Establish the actual required period in the permit before beginning.

Worked case: the welder finishes at 15:00 and the watch is reassigned at 15:05. The short elapsed time does not satisfy the covered half-hour minimum, and the site may require more. Maintain the required watch, inspect affected and adjacent areas and record the actual completion and release. If an inaccessible void remains suspect, do not sign a clean handback merely because no flame is visible. Escalate for a suitable inspection and continued controls.`,[
 ['A solvent transfer begins near permitted hot work. What follows?','Stop and reassess the changed conditions.','Continue under the original signature.','Raise the alarm setpoint.','Shorten the watch period.','New release conditions can invalidate the permit basis.'],
 ['Under the cited general-industry welding rule, minimum post-work watch is?','At least half an hour, with longer applicable requirements honored.','Five minutes everywhere.','None after the torch stops.','A universal four hours from an unverified edition.','The OSHA provision sets a minimum; actual site requirements can be more protective.'],
 ['A concealed void remains suspect at handback. What next?','Continue appropriate control and obtain inspection.','Sign it clear without evidence.','Delete the concern.','Rely on the absence of visible flame.','Smoldering can occur outside the visible work area.']]],
 ['Evaluate the permit survey and watch response',`Use a cold training setup with a workpiece, photographs or a marked drawing of adjacent spaces. The learner identifies ignition sources, combustible paths, equipment isolation, monitoring needs and the watch positions. Include a hidden combustible behind a partition and an opening to a lower level. The learner must explain what must change before authorization; merely filling every permit box is insufficient.

For the watch role, demonstrate access to the alarm and the appropriate training extinguisher. Practice the stop signal and a safe withdrawal decision. Any live-fire exercise belongs under a suitably designed and supervised training program, not an improvised flame in a work area. The evaluator should assess the learner's limits as carefully as their confidence.

Passing requires complete area survey, effective watch coverage, correct stop decisions, alarm activation and a realistic post-work handback. Ignoring an unseen exposure, leaving active work unwatched, entering smoke beyond role or falsifying the watch duration is a critical failure. Stop, teach and reassess using a different layout. Record whether the learner was assessed as operator, watch or permit authority; these roles are not interchangeable. Keep the online completion, observed practical result and actual hot-work permit separate so a course completion cannot be mistaken for permission to ignite a tool on any site.`,[
 ['Which exercise tests survey quality?','Finding a concealed combustible and an unobserved lower level.','Counting permit pages only.','Naming the welder’s employer.','Timing the fastest signature.','The survey must identify actual ignition paths.'],
 ['Which is a critical failure?','Falsifying the post-work watch duration.','Stopping for a changed release.','Requesting another watch position.','Activating an alarm.','A false record can conceal an unprotected smoldering-fire period.'],
 ['What does online completion replace?','Neither the practical role assessment nor the actual permit.','Every site hot-work permit.','Every fire-brigade qualification.','All equipment-specific training.','Knowledge, role performance and task authorization are distinct.']]]
]);

program('OG30',[osha('1926.651'),osha('1926.652')],
 ['Utility and soil decisions','Protective-system selection','Inspection and emergency changes','Competent-person evidence'],
 ['Competent-person appointment requires actual capability and authority. Site soil analysis, utilities and engineering designs cannot be established online.'],[
 ['Locate services and assess ground as changing conditions',`A utility drawing is planning information, not proof of the exact position of every service. Before excavation, determine expected utility locations, contact the relevant owners and follow the required locating arrangements. As work approaches the estimated location, determine the exact position by safe and acceptable means. Protect, support or remove exposed utilities as necessary. A missing mark does not establish an empty corridor.

Soil and site conditions affect collapse risk. Previously disturbed ground, water, vibration, adjacent foundations and surcharge loads can weaken the excavation. A competent person must identify existing and predictable hazards and have authority to correct them promptly. A training card without that capability and authority does not meet the role definition. Soil classification and protective-system decisions require the applicable analysis and design information, not a glance from the truck window.

Worked case: yesterday's dry excavation is wet after overnight rain, and a loaded truck now parks near the edge. The original inspection no longer describes the conditions. Keep workers out pending inspection and corrective measures. Evaluate water, surcharge, soil behavior and the protective system together. Do not send a worker inside to inspect a crack from below. A trench can collapse without giving a person time to escape.`,[
 ['An unmarked corridor proves no buried service exists. Correct?','No; locating and verification obligations remain.','Yes if the drawing is recent.','Yes if the ground is dry.','Yes after one shallow scrape.','Records and markings do not establish every exact utility location.'],
 ['Rain and an edge load appear overnight. What next?','Reinspect and correct hazards before entry.','Use yesterday’s approval.','Send a worker to inspect from below.','Continue until a wall moves.','Changed conditions can invalidate the previous protection basis.'],
 ['What distinguishes a competent person from a course graduate?','Hazard-recognition capability and authority for prompt correction.','A more decorative card.','Longer attendance alone.','Ownership of a shovel.','The role requires both competence and real authority.']]],
 ['Select protection without inventing a design',`Protect employees from cave-ins through an adequate protective system except where the standard's specific exceptions apply. For excavations under five feet, the exception depends on a competent person's examination finding no indication of a potential cave-in; shallow does not automatically mean safe. Stable rock is another specific condition, not a description to apply casually to hard soil.

Sloping, benching, shoring and shielding have different functions and limitations. A trench shield protects within its designed area; it does not necessarily prevent surrounding soil movement or protect a worker outside it. Follow the appropriate tabulated data, manufacturer's limits or engineered design. Keep the required information available and do not combine components into an unapproved arrangement. Excavations deeper than twenty feet require protective systems designed by a registered professional engineer or based on approved tabulated data as allowed by the standard.

Worked case: a shield is available but too short for the work area, so the crew proposes standing beyond its end for only a minute. That is exposure outside the protection, not an acceptable extension of its rating. Change the system or work method before entry. Likewise, moving a shield, working below its bottom or using damaged components needs the applicable design conditions and competent review; familiarity with the brand cannot supply missing design permission.`,[
 ['Does every excavation under five feet need no cave-in protection?','No; the exception requires the specified competent-person finding.','Yes automatically.','Yes if the job is brief.','Yes if a ladder is present.','Shallow excavations can still have cave-in hazards.'],
 ['A worker stands beyond a shield’s protected area. Is the shield sufficient?','No; the worker is outside its protection.','Yes for one minute.','Yes with a hard hat.','Yes if the shield is new.','Protection must cover the actual exposure position.'],
 ['Can missing design data be replaced by field guesswork?','No; use the applicable approved design route.','Yes if components fit.','Yes if the foreman agrees.','Yes below the road surface.','Protective systems require a defensible design basis.']]],
 ['Access, inspections and a safe emergency response',`For trenches four feet or deeper, provide the required safe egress so workers need not travel more than twenty-five feet laterally to reach it. Access is not cave-in protection, and a ladder must remain usable within the protected work arrangement. Keep spoil and equipment from falling or rolling into the excavation, using the required setback or retaining means. Evaluate mobile-equipment warning systems and adjacent structures, not only the trench wall.

Where hazardous atmospheres can reasonably be expected, apply the required atmospheric testing and controls. Water accumulation needs suitable precautions and monitoring. Daily inspections by the competent person occur before work and as needed during the shift, with inspections after rainstorms or other hazard-increasing events. Remove exposed employees when hazardous conditions are found until necessary precautions are taken.

Worked case: a worker becomes unresponsive in a trench beside a leaking service. Coworkers must not jump in. Activate the emergency plan, isolate others from the area and communicate the suspected utility and atmospheric hazards. Rescue requires appropriate trained personnel and equipment. A cave-in can create secondary collapse, and an atmosphere can incapacitate rescuers. Preserve the location information for responders without turning a phone photo into an excuse to approach an unstable edge.`,[
 ['For a trench four feet or deeper, required egress is within what lateral travel?','Twenty-five feet.','One hundred feet.','Any distance if a ladder exists.','Only at the far end.','The excavation rule specifies the egress travel limit.'],
 ['A hazardous condition is found during inspection. What happens?','Remove exposed workers until necessary precautions are taken.','Finish the current task first.','Add a warning to tomorrow’s form.','Let workers decide individually.','Inspection must trigger corrective action, not merely documentation.'],
 ['A person collapses near a leaking utility in the trench. What next?','Activate trained emergency response from safety.','Jump in immediately.','Use an ordinary dust mask to enter.','Send the smallest worker.','Uncontrolled rescue can create more casualties.']]],
 ['Assess the person who will make the field decisions',`Use site photographs, utility information, soil samples where appropriate and a protective-system data package. The learner identifies the scope, explains the evidence needed for soil and system decisions and identifies conditions outside their authority. A competent-person candidate must do more than recognize a trench in a photograph: they must connect observations to prompt corrective action and have actual authority to carry it out.

Present a wet excavation, an edge surcharge, a missing access point and a shield with incomplete data. Ask for a sequence of decisions. The correct response prevents entry while the deficiencies are resolved, obtains engineering or utility input where required and establishes inspection triggers. No learner should enter an unprotected excavation to demonstrate confidence. Field observation must occur from safe positions under a controlled training plan.

The rubric covers utilities, soil and water, protective-system limits, access, spoil and traffic, atmosphere, inspections and emergency boundaries. Allowing entry with unresolved collapse hazards, inventing soil classification or bypassing missing design data is a critical failure. Document the original result, remediation and reassessment. The employer separately confirms the person's authority, assigned site scope and access to necessary resources. An online certificate may support that decision but cannot appoint a competent person, certify a soil analysis or supply a professional engineer's design.`,[
 ['A shield’s data package is incomplete. What is the correct evaluation response?','Withhold entry and obtain the required design information.','Assume all shields are equivalent.','Test it with workers inside.','Ignore it if painted green.','Missing design limits prevent reliable system selection.'],
 ['What must the employer establish beyond course knowledge?','Actual corrective authority and capability for the assigned scope.','Only a new job title.','Only a certificate frame.','Only years of employment.','Competent-person status is operational, not a title alone.'],
 ['Which record cannot this online course issue?','A professional engineer’s site-specific protective-system design.','A knowledge assessment score.','A completion date.','A record of lesson attendance.','Engineering design requires its actual qualified author and site basis.']]]
]);
program('OG08',[osha('1910.147'),osha('1910.333')],
 ['Scope and role selection','Multiple energy sources','Group transfer and restoration','Equipment-specific observation'],
 ['1910.147 excludes oil and gas well drilling/servicing; determine governing requirements. Actual isolation procedures and observed equipment proficiency are employer-controlled.'],[
 ['Identify the governing task and every energy source',`An authorized employee establishes and applies energy control for servicing. An affected employee operates or works around equipment that is controlled and must understand the prohibition against restarting it. Job title alone does not establish either role. First determine the applicable work scope: OSHA 1910.147 expressly excludes oil and gas well drilling and servicing, among other activities. That exclusion is not a finding that unexpected energy is safe. The employer must identify applicable rules and implement the equipment's safe energy-control procedure. Electrical utilization work also has separate electrical safe-work requirements.

For a shop conveyor, draw an energy inventory: motor supply, pneumatic actuator, elevated carriage, spring tension and material that can roll or fall. Trace the energy to isolating devices, not merely stop buttons or control screens. A variable-frequency drive can retain electrical charge after shutdown. A valve can leak and pressure can rebuild. The procedure must address these mechanisms rather than listing only the most visible disconnect.

Worked case: a stopped hydraulic lift remains raised. Switching off the pump does not prevent gravity descent. The authorized worker needs the specified support and stored-energy control before placing any body part beneath the lift. An affected worker reports the condition and stays clear; taking this course does not authorize improvising a support or operating another person's lock.`,[
 ['Does 1910.147 automatically govern well-servicing operations?','No; its express exclusion requires a scope-specific determination.','Yes, without exception.','Only if a worker signs a quiz.','It governs only when convenient.','The standard explicitly excludes oil and gas well drilling and servicing.'],
 ['A raised lift has its pump switched off. Which hazard remains?','Gravity and stored hydraulic energy.','None because the motor stopped.','Only noise.','Only chemical labeling.','Shutdown does not eliminate all stored energy.'],
 ['Which item normally provides energy isolation?','The identified energy-isolating device.','A touchscreen stop command.','An emergency-stop button alone.','A supervisor’s radio message.','Control-circuit commands do not substitute for physical energy isolation.']]],
 ['Prove the isolated state before servicing',`The equipment procedure determines the sequence: prepare and notify affected people, shut down normally, isolate each energy source, apply the assigned locks or permitted tagout controls, control stored energy, and verify the protected state. Verification must match the hazard. A start attempt may test a control function but cannot prove absence of electrical voltage. Electrical testing must be done by a qualified person using suitable methods and instruments. Return operating controls to their safe position after a verification attempt.

Worked case: an air cylinder has a closed supply valve and a zero gauge, but a second feed enters downstream. Trace both feeds against the current equipment arrangement. The single gauge may be isolated, damaged or reading a different pocket. Do not loosen a fitting to see whether pressure remains. Use the approved verification and dissipation methods, and reassess if energy can reaccumulate during work. A temporary zero is not a permanent safe state.

Tags communicate a prohibition but do not physically restrain operation as a lock does. Where tagout is used under an applicable rule, its limitations and required additional protection must be addressed. Never remove another person's device because the schedule is late. The employer's exceptional-removal procedure has specific safeguards; informal permission from a coworker does not replace it.`,[
 ['A start attempt proves absence of voltage. Correct?','No; electrical verification requires the appropriate qualified test.','Yes for all motors.','Yes when the screen is dark.','Yes if performed twice.','Control response and electrical absence-of-voltage testing are different checks.'],
 ['A second pressure feed is discovered. What next?','Revise isolation and verification before exposure.','Ignore it if the first gauge reads zero.','Loosen a fitting to test it.','Rely on gloves.','Every hazardous energy path must be addressed.'],
 ['What if pressure may reaccumulate?','Use the procedure’s continuing control and verification.','Assume the first zero is permanent.','Remove the warning tag.','Finish faster without monitoring.','Stored energy can return even after initial dissipation.']]],
 ['Protect the individual through group work and restoration',`Group isolation must give each exposed worker protection equivalent to the applicable individual system. A lead worker's lock is not a reason for everyone else to work without the required personal protection. The procedure establishes responsibility, lockbox or other arrangement, verification and each person's entry into and release from the protection system. Contractors and the host exchange their energy-control procedures so that one crew's restart cannot expose another.

At shift change, the incoming worker's protection must become effective before the outgoing protection is removed in the manner required by the procedure. A verbal promise that nobody will start the machine leaves a gap. Record unfinished work, installed blocks, changed configuration and any remaining people in the work zone. A shift roster alone does not establish that the physical controls transferred correctly.

Worked case: one fitter has left the site with a lock still installed. The supervisor cannot simply cut it. Use the employer's documented exceptional-removal process, including its checks and notification safeguards. Before normal restoration, inspect the equipment, remove nonessential items, confirm personnel are safely positioned, remove devices through the proper authority and notify affected employees. Temporary energization for testing requires a controlled sequence and renewed protection before servicing resumes.`,[
 ['What must shift transfer avoid?','A gap in individual protection.','Recording unfinished work.','Briefing incoming personnel.','Confirming who remains exposed.','Protection must remain effective across the handover.'],
 ['A departed fitter’s lock blocks startup. What is appropriate?','Follow the documented exceptional-removal safeguards.','Cut it on a verbal request.','Let any coworker remove it.','Restart around it.','Another person’s device cannot be bypassed informally.'],
 ['After temporary testing, servicing resumes under what condition?','Required isolation and personal protection are reestablished.','The successful test replaces isolation.','Only a warning sign is needed.','The supervisor watches the controls.','Testing does not waive protection for renewed exposure.']]],
 ['Observe an authorized worker on the actual procedure',`Evaluate on safe training equipment or an appropriately controlled actual task with a qualified evaluator. Give the learner the equipment-specific procedure and ask them to identify each energy source, device and verification step. Include a discrepancy such as an unlabeled secondary supply. A competent response is to stop and resolve it, not to guess the nearest disconnect. The evaluator must not deliberately expose the learner to an uncontrolled live hazard.

The rubric follows the whole sequence: notification, shutdown, isolation, personal device application, stored-energy control, verification, work-boundary protection and restoration. Add a group-work or shift-change scenario for workers assigned those duties. Affected employees need a different assessment: recognize isolation, avoid restart, respect devices and report changes. Do not make affected workers demonstrate electrical tests beyond their qualifications.

Wrong-equipment isolation, omitted stored energy, entry before verification or unauthorized device removal is a critical failure. Stop, instruct, practice and reassess. Record the original error and corrected performance, equipment identity, procedure revision, evaluator and date. Under 1910.147, periodic inspection of the energy-control procedure is a distinct at-least-annual requirement performed by an authorized employee other than those using the procedure being inspected. A training certificate cannot be relabeled as that inspection. The inspection must examine the applicable procedure and implementation.`,[
 ['Which defect should stop the demonstration?','An unidentified secondary supply.','A clearly legible procedure.','A verified lock identity.','A complete handover.','An unresolved energy source prevents a defensible isolation.'],
 ['Is annual procedure inspection the same as learner training?','No; it is a distinct required review.','Yes whenever a quiz passes.','Yes if the trainer signs twice.','Only the document title differs.','Procedure inspection evaluates implementation and has separate requirements.'],
 ['Which record supports a bounded authorization?','Equipment, procedure revision and observed role performance.','Only attendance.','An unlimited electrical license.','A claim of competence on every machine.','Authorization must match what was actually demonstrated.']]]
]);

program('OG09',[osha('1926.503'),osha('1910.30'),osha('1926.502')],
 ['Protection selection','Clearance and compatibility reasoning','Rescue and dropped objects','Fall-user demonstration'],
 ['Manufacturer-specific configuration, anchorage approval, rescue capability and observed practice remain separate. No rope-access or universal competent-person qualification is granted.'],[
 ['Choose a system that prevents the actual exposure',`Start with the work: can the valve be operated from ground level, can a permanent platform be used, or can guarded access remove the need to approach an edge? Prevention and collective protection generally reduce dependence on a worker making repeated connections. A restraint system prevents reaching the fall location. A fall-arrest system permits a fall and then stops it, so it creates clearance and rescue questions that restraint may avoid. These are different functions, not interchangeable names for wearing a harness.

A fictional mechanic must service a unit beside an open mezzanine edge. A harness stored nearby is not a complete protection system. The plan must identify access, edge exposure, approved anchorage, connectors, equipment compatibility, clearance and rescue. Select the applicable general-industry or construction rule; thresholds and task-specific provisions should not be guessed from a single remembered height. Other hazards, including dangerous equipment below, can affect the protection decision.

Inspect the actual travel path. A worker may be protected at the final position but exposed while climbing over a rail or crossing an opening. Maintain the planned protection through transitions. If the task changes so that a restraint line can now reach the edge, stop and reassess; the earlier classification no longer describes the arrangement.`,[
 ['What is the purpose of restraint?','Prevent reaching the fall hazard.','Stop a free fall after it begins.','Replace an anchorage.','Provide rescue after suspension.','Restraint limits access to the edge.'],
 ['A harness is available. Is the system complete?','No; compatible anchorage, connectors and planning are still needed.','Yes for any height.','Yes if it looks new.','Yes if a coworker holds the line.','A harness alone does not control a fall.'],
 ['A changed line length now permits reaching the edge. What next?','Reassess the protection arrangement.','Continue calling it restraint.','Ignore the change.','Remove the edge warning.','System function depends on the actual geometry.']]],
 ['Calculate clearance using the actual equipment data',`Fall clearance is the space needed to stop a fall without striking a lower level or obstruction. Consider the allowed free fall, deployment or deceleration, harness and connection effects, worker position, system deflection and required margin using the manufacturer's method. Do not add an invented universal number to every lanyard. Self-retracting devices have specific applications, including limitations for leading edges and anchorage position. A unit suitable overhead may not be suitable at foot level or over a sharp edge.

Worked arithmetic, for training only: an approved planning worksheet gives 6 feet of free fall, 3.5 feet of deceleration, 5 feet for the worker and connection geometry and a 2-foot margin. That illustration totals 16.5 feet. If the actual available space is 12 feet, the illustrated arrangement cannot be accepted. The solution is a different approved work method or system, not subtracting the margin. Actual equipment instructions determine which terms apply and where clearance is measured.

Swing falls also matter. An anchorage far to one side can allow a pendulum path into a column even if vertical clearance is sufficient. Evaluate edges, nearby structures and system compatibility. Only the appropriate qualified or competent authority should approve the anchorage and design within the governing requirements; a worker's weight or a solid-looking pipe is not an anchorage rating.`,[
 ['Illustrated clearance is 6 + 3.5 + 5 + 2 feet. What is the total?','16.5 feet.','12 feet.','11.5 feet.','8 feet.','All specified terms must be retained in this hypothetical worksheet.'],
 ['Only 12 feet is available for that 16.5-foot arrangement. What follows?','Select another approved arrangement before work.','Remove the margin.','Use a heavier harness.','Accept it because the fall is unlikely.','Insufficient clearance makes that arrangement unsuitable.'],
 ['Adequate vertical clearance removes which hazard automatically?','None; swing and edge hazards still need assessment.','All pendulum impacts.','All sharp-edge damage.','All anchorage failures.','Clearance is only one component of system suitability.']]],
 ['Plan rescue and protect the area below',`A stopped fall leaves an emergency: the worker may be injured, suspended and unable to self-rescue. The rescue method must be feasible from the actual location, with trained people, suitable equipment and timely access. A generic statement to call emergency services does not establish that they can reach a suspended worker behind process piping. Coordinate the plan before work and make sure it remains available during the shift.

Dropped tools and materials create a separate exposure. Secure items with suitable systems, control materials near edges and establish protection for people below. Do not attach a heavy tool tether to a harness point that is not intended for it. Toe boards, screening, exclusion areas and overhead protection depend on the work and governing requirements. A barrier must actually exclude pedestrians; a cone hidden behind a truck is not effective separation.

Worked case: the planned rescue platform is moved to another job after the crew goes aloft. That changes readiness. Suspend the exposed task and restore a feasible rescue arrangement before continuing. If a fall occurs, activate the plan and prevent improvised entry into another hazard. Equipment subjected to a fall or impact loading must be removed from service and handled under the applicable inspection and disposition requirements. A visible absence of damage is not a return-to-service decision.`,[
 ['The assigned rescue platform leaves the site. What should happen?','Reassess and restore rescue readiness before continuing exposure.','Continue because no fall occurred.','Replace it with a phone number alone.','Ask workers to climb faster.','The approved rescue method must remain feasible.'],
 ['Who is protected by dropped-object controls?','People below and around the work.','Only the harness wearer.','Only the equipment owner.','Only the evaluator.','Falling objects create a distinct exposure area.'],
 ['Fall-loaded equipment looks undamaged. What next?','Remove it from service for required disposition.','Immediately reuse it.','Paint over the mark.','Let the wearer vote.','Appearance alone cannot establish post-fall integrity.']]],
 ['Evaluate users on a controlled ground-level station',`The evaluator supplies the actual equipment instructions and a safe ground-level training station. The learner inspects webbing, stitching, connectors, labels and indicators, identifies rejection conditions, dons and adjusts the harness and demonstrates compatible connection. The station can simulate edges and transitions without requiring an exposed climb. Use the manufacturer's inspection criteria rather than invented limits for cuts, wear or corrosion.

Have the learner explain a clearance worksheet and identify a swing-fall obstruction on a drawing. Then present a changed anchorage or a missing rescue resource. The learner must stop and route the issue to the proper authority. For authorized elevated practice, establish real protection and supervision; a training label does not exempt the exercise from fall requirements.

Pass criteria are correct inspection, fit, connection, travel planning, limitation recognition, dropped-object controls and emergency communication. Connecting to an unapproved point, ignoring a rejected component, misclassifying arrest as restraint or starting without feasible rescue is a critical failure. Provide targeted practice and reassess with a new configuration. Record the system, manufacturer, task, evaluator and restrictions. A construction training certification and an employer work authorization are distinct records. Neither a successful quiz nor this demonstration grants scaffold-erector or rope-access qualifications outside the assessed scope.`,[
 ['Where can basic harness skills safely be assessed?','A controlled ground-level training station.','An unguarded edge without backup.','An unknown anchorage above traffic.','A live fall test using the learner.','Ground-level simulation can show the required handling without needless exposure.'],
 ['Which observation is a critical failure?','Connecting to an unapproved anchorage.','Rejecting damaged webbing.','Checking the clearance method.','Requesting rescue confirmation.','An unapproved anchorage invalidates protection.'],
 ['What defines the practical authorization?','The system, task and skills actually observed.','Every height-related trade.','The course title alone.','An unlimited rope-access endorsement.','Role and equipment boundaries must remain explicit.']]]
]);

program('OG11',[osha('1910.178')],
 ['Truck-specific stability','Travel docks and pedestrians','Inspection energy and fueling','Workplace operator evaluation'],
 ['Formal instruction is only one element. Actual truck-type practical training and workplace evaluation by an appropriately experienced evaluator are required.'],[
 ['Read the capacity plate as a conditional limit',`A forklift's capacity applies to the truck configuration, load center and other conditions on its data plate. An attachment changes the geometry and may change capacity; do not assume the truck's old maximum still applies. The load's center of gravity matters as well as weight. A long crate or an off-center load may create a larger overturning moment than a compact load of the same mass. Keep the load stable, appropriately supported and within the approved rating.

Worked reasoning: two pallets weigh the same, but one extends much farther forward. The longer load moves the combined center of gravity toward the front tipping boundary. The operator must obtain the actual capacity information for that load center and configuration, not simply compare weight with the largest number on the truck. Tilting, braking, turning and uneven ground add dynamic effects; a stationary arithmetic estimate is not an operating authorization.

Understand the truck type. A sit-down counterbalance truck, a reach truck and a powered pallet truck have different controls, stability characteristics and operator positions. Read the manual and receive practical instruction for the actual class. Never use personnel on forks as a substitute for an approved personnel-lifting arrangement. Keep people clear of elevated loads and pinch points, and do not permit unauthorized riders.`,[
 ['Same weight, longer forward load: what changes?','Load center and overturning moment.','Nothing relevant.','Only paint clearance.','Only fuel consumption.','Load geometry affects capacity and stability.'],
 ['A new attachment is installed. What must be checked?','Approved capacity information for the changed configuration.','Only the old maximum rating.','Only the attachment color.','Only the operator’s years of service.','Attachments can alter capacity and operating characteristics.'],
 ['Training on one truck proves proficiency on all types. Correct?','No; type-specific differences require appropriate instruction and evaluation.','Yes after one quiz.','Yes if all have forks.','Yes if the company owns them.','Truck types differ in controls, stability and operation.']]],
 ['Travel with a route that protects pedestrians',`Before moving, inspect the route for people, blind corners, surface defects, slopes, overhead restrictions and dock edges. Keep travel speed low enough to stop safely under the actual conditions. Use the truck's prescribed travel position and maintain a clear view. If a load blocks forward vision, follow the applicable safe travel method and use assistance where necessary; do not guess that a pedestrian will hear the truck.

At an intersection, sound the warning as required, slow and ensure the way is clear. A horn communicates but does not create separation. The site should provide defined pedestrian routes and controlled crossing points. When a spotter is used, agree signals and stop if contact is lost. Never allow a person between the truck or load and a fixed surface where they can be crushed.

Worked case: a trailer appears ready, but its restraint status and floor capacity are unknown. Hold loading until the required vehicle restraint, support, dockboard capacity and floor condition are verified. A driver in the cab is not equivalent to an effective restraint system. On grades, follow the truck instructions and applicable load-orientation rules; do not turn across a slope simply to face the preferred direction. The operator must know the actual ramp limits rather than invent a safe angle.`,[
 ['A load blocks forward view. What is required?','Use the approved safe travel method with adequate visibility.','Drive faster to clear the area.','Rely only on the horn.','Assume pedestrians will move.','The operator must maintain safe visibility and control.'],
 ['Trailer restraint and floor condition are unknown. What next?','Hold loading until readiness is verified.','Enter slowly as a test.','Ask a worker to stand behind the trailer.','Use a lighter first pallet as proof.','Dock and trailer failures cannot be tested safely by entering them.'],
 ['A spotter disappears from view. What should the operator do?','Stop until communication and safe positioning are restored.','Continue by memory.','Increase horn use and proceed.','Ask a pedestrian to guess the signals.','Loss of the agreed communication safeguard requires stopping.']]],
 ['Inspect, park and service within the operator role',`Before use, examine the truck under the employer's checklist and manufacturer's instructions. Check visible structural condition, forks and attachments, tires, controls, brakes, steering, warning devices, restraint and fluid or energy-system problems. A defect affecting safe operation requires removal from service and authorized repair. Do not defeat an interlock or continue with a known brake problem because the travel distance is short.

Parking means placing the truck in the required safe condition, including lowering the load-engaging means, neutralizing controls, setting the brake and shutting down as applicable. Prevent unintended movement on inclines. A raised fork creates a strike hazard even when the operator intends to return immediately. Keys and access controls should prevent unauthorized use; leaving the truck running does not transfer supervision to a nearby coworker.

Fueling and charging present their own hazards: ignition sources, battery electrolyte, ventilation, electrical energy and heavy components. Use the designated location, procedures and assigned protective equipment. LPG cylinder exchange, battery handling and charging need equipment-specific instruction. A charging connector is not a place for improvised repair. Worked case: a worker detects a fuel odor after exchange. Stop, isolate the truck from use as the procedure requires and report for authorized correction; do not start it to see whether the odor clears.`,[
 ['A brake defect is found before a short move. What follows?','Remove the truck from service for authorized correction.','Use it slowly.','Assign a spotter as a brake substitute.','Move only an empty pallet.','A short distance does not make defective brakes acceptable.'],
 ['What should happen to forks during proper parking?','Place them in the prescribed lowered safe position.','Leave them at head height.','Balance a pallet on the tips.','Ask a coworker to watch them.','Raised forks create a predictable strike hazard.'],
 ['Fuel odor follows a cylinder exchange. What next?','Stop use and follow the fuel-system defect procedure.','Start the engine to clear fumes.','Check with a flame.','Ignore it if the gauge works.','Potential leakage requires controlled investigation without ignition.']]],
 ['Evaluate real workplace performance',`OSHA's covered powered-industrial-truck training combines formal instruction, practical training and evaluation of workplace performance. The evaluator needs the knowledge, training and experience to train operators and evaluate competence. A written score does not satisfy the practical elements. The employer's certification identifies the operator, training date, evaluation date and trainer or evaluator identity.

Build the practical route around the actual workplace: inspection, safe mounting, controls, unloaded maneuvering, a representative rated load, stacking or placement, a blind intersection, pedestrian separation and safe parking. Include docks or ramps only where they are part of the assignment and can be safely assessed. The evaluator retains control of the exercise and stops unsafe movement before harm occurs. Do not stage a pedestrian surprise in the truck's path.

Passing requires correct load and capacity decisions, stable handling, observation, controlled speed, separation and recognition of a stop condition. Collision, ignored pedestrian exclusion, travel with an unstable load or bypassing a safety defect is a critical failure. Train and reevaluate before authorization. Evaluate operator performance at least every three years under the covered rule. Refresher training and evaluation also follow unsafe operation, an accident or near miss, a deficient evaluation, assignment to a different truck type or a workplace change affecting safe operation. Record the trigger and task scope instead of automatically renewing every truck class.`,[
 ['Which training element cannot be replaced by an online quiz?','Practical training and workplace evaluation.','Reading the manual.','Learning warning symbols.','Reviewing the training plan.','The regulation requires observed operation as well as instruction.'],
 ['How often is covered operator performance evaluated at minimum?','At least every three years.','Only at hiring.','Every ten years.','Only after injury.','Specified trigger events can require earlier refresher and evaluation.'],
 ['A different truck type is assigned. What is required?','Relevant refresher training and evaluation.','Automatic transfer of all privileges.','Only changing the badge title.','No action if both trucks have forks.','Different types may materially change safe operation.']]]
]);

program('OG26',[osha('1910.1200')],
 ['Product and label interpretation','SDS task decisions','Nonroutine work and employer interfaces','Workplace teach-back'],
 ['Actual chemical inventory, current SDSs and written workplace program must be supplied by employer. Applicable HazCom transition obligations require current workplace review.'],[
 ['Connect the container to the actual chemical',`A product identifier connects the container label, inventory and safety data sheet. Similar trade names do not establish identical composition or hazards. Before transfer or use, confirm the exact product and readable information. A shipped hazardous-chemical label provides elements such as the identifier, signal word, hazard statements, pictograms, precautionary statements and supplier information. Workplace labeling follows the applicable rule and employer system; workers must understand that system rather than assume every secondary container needs the identical shipped-label design.

Worked case: two clear liquids sit in unmarked spray bottles beside a parts washer. One is water-based cleaner and the other is flammable solvent. Appearance and smell are not reliable identification methods. Stop use, segregate under the workplace procedure and obtain positive identification and appropriate labeling. Do not pour one into the other or perform a trial cleaning to find out.

The immediate-use exception is narrow: it concerns a portable container under the control of, and used only by, the employee who transferred the chemical within that work shift. Leaving a bottle for the next crew is not immediate use. Labels and SDSs communicate hazards, but the worker also needs the actual location, process and protective measures taught by the employer. An inventory entry cannot substitute for a legible container identity at the point of use.`,[
 ['Two clear unlabeled liquids are found. What next?','Stop use and establish identity through the workplace procedure.','Smell each bottle closely.','Mix them to see a reaction.','Assume both are water.','Appearance cannot establish chemical identity.'],
 ['A portable bottle is left for another shift. Is immediate-use treatment justified?','No; the narrow immediate-use conditions are not met.','Yes if small.','Yes if transparent.','Yes if the cap is closed.','Immediate use does not cover leaving the chemical for another worker.'],
 ['Which identifier should connect label and SDS?','The actual product identifier.','The bottle color.','The supplier’s logo alone.','The last worker’s name.','Matching product identity prevents applying the wrong hazard information.']]],
 ['Read the SDS as a set of task decisions',`Use the SDS to answer a question about work, not simply to prove that a file exists. Identification and hazard information establish the substance and principal dangers. First-aid, fire, accidental-release, handling and storage sections support emergency and prevention planning. Exposure-control and PPE information informs selection, while physical properties and stability/reactivity information help evaluate ignition, incompatibility and release behavior. The employer must make SDS information readily accessible during each shift.

A glove recommendation needs context: material, thickness, contact duration and task affect protection. A generic statement to wear gloves does not establish that every disposable glove resists the solvent. Ventilation recommendations must become an effective arrangement at the actual source. A respirator recommendation does not itself satisfy medical evaluation, fit testing or selection under the respiratory program.

Worked case: an outdoor wipe-down becomes heated spraying inside a small enclosure. Heating and aerosol generation can change inhalation and ignition risks. Review the new process, ventilation, exposure assessment, compatibility and emergency arrangements before proceeding. The old SDS is still relevant, but the old task assessment may not be. The worker should locate the relevant SDS sections and explain the operational change, then obtain the required qualified decisions instead of inventing exposure limits or cartridge selections.`,[
 ['Heated spraying replaces outdoor wiping. What must be reviewed?','The changed exposure and ignition conditions and controls.','Only the purchase order.','Nothing if the product is unchanged.','Only container size.','The process can change risk without changing chemical identity.'],
 ['“Wear gloves” establishes which specific glove?','None without compatibility and task information.','Any disposable glove.','The cheapest glove.','Any glove of the right color.','Chemical resistance depends on material and use conditions.'],
 ['An SDS recommends a respirator. What remains necessary?','The employer respiratory-program selection and prerequisites.','Only buying a mask.','Only signing the SDS.','Nothing else.','An SDS recommendation is not a complete respiratory program.']]],
 ['Nonroutine work and contractor exchange',`Routine use may occur in a closed process; maintenance can open the same chemical to workers. Before line breaking, vessel cleaning or residue removal, identify what remains in the equipment, the possible reaction products and the methods that prevent exposure. Process labels or pipe markings do not prove that a drained line is empty. Coordinate isolation, verification and any permit requirements with the responsible operators.

The workplace program must address how employees learn about nonroutine hazards and how employers exchange information where other employers' workers may be exposed. A contractor needs access to relevant SDSs, precautionary measures and the labeling system. The host needs information about chemicals introduced by the contractor that can affect its workers. Passing a general course does not complete this two-way exchange.

Worked case: a cleaning contractor brings an acid product near a bleach-containing residue. The crew pauses because mixing may release dangerous gas. Confirm product and residue identities, prevent mixing and obtain an approved method. Do not test compatibility in a bucket. Emergency action also stays within role: an ordinary worker reports a spill, warns others and follows evacuation or isolation instructions. Cleanup of an unknown release belongs to personnel with the assigned training, equipment and response scope, not whoever finds an absorbent pad first.`,[
 ['Who exchanges chemical hazard information at a multi-employer site?','The affected employers through their coordinated program.','Only the delivery driver.','Only the course platform.','Nobody if SDSs exist online.','Workers need the actual workplace hazards and precautionary arrangements.'],
 ['Acid cleaner may contact bleach residue. What next?','Prevent mixing and obtain an approved compatible method.','Try a small bucket mixture.','Increase the dose.','Rely only on goggles.','Incompatible products can produce hazardous reactions.'],
 ['Who should clean an unknown spill?','Personnel assigned and equipped for that response scope.','The first worker nearby.','Any worker with absorbent pads.','Anyone with a HazCom quiz pass.','HazCom awareness does not establish specialist spill-response competence.']]],
 ['Demonstrate the workplace program through teach-back',`Use actual site labels and SDSs for a safe classroom exercise, with empty or sealed training containers. The learner matches the product identifier, explains the label warnings in their own words, locates the SDS without delay and identifies the controls for an assigned task. Ask where the written program is available and who resolves a missing label, unfamiliar product or changed hazard. The exercise should use a language and literacy approach the worker understands.

Give the learner a nonroutine maintenance scenario and ask which information must be obtained before work. A useful answer connects the chemical to exposure routes, incompatible materials, ventilation or enclosure, PPE limitations and emergency actions. Reading every SDS heading aloud is not the same as applying it. Offer an opportunity for questions about the actual job.

Passing requires correct product matching, usable access to information, recognition of the task hazard and an appropriate control or escalation decision. Using an unidentified product, intentionally mixing incompatible materials or claiming a dust mask protects against every gas is a critical error. Teach the missing concept and reassess using another product or task. Record the worker, date, hazards covered and employer-provided workplace information. Train at initial assignment and when a new chemical hazard is introduced. A new brand with the same hazards and an entirely new hazard are different training decisions; review the facts rather than automatically treating every purchase identically.`,[
 ['Which demonstration is stronger than reciting SDS headings?','Applying the relevant sections to the assigned task.','Memorizing the document length.','Naming the supplier’s city.','Counting pictograms only.','Training must enable practical hazard understanding.'],
 ['When is additional HazCom instruction needed?','When a new chemical hazard is introduced.','Only every decade.','Only after an injury.','Never after initial hiring.','The training obligation follows introduction of new hazards.'],
 ['Which error prevents a satisfactory teach-back?','Using a product whose identity is unknown.','Asking for a translated explanation.','Finding the written program.','Reporting a missing label.','Unidentified chemical use prevents a reliable hazard decision.']]]
]);
program('OG03',[osha('1910.134'),'https://www.osha.gov/hydrogen-sulfide/hazards'],
 ['Release pathways and detector limits','Escape route case','Respiratory and rescue role decisions','Safe practical assessment'],
 ['Not a Veriforce H2S Clear credential. Site H2S plan, exposure assessment, detector-specific demonstration and assigned respirator prerequisites remain employer deliverables.'],[
 ['A tank reading is a measurement, not a forecast',`A production tank contains liquid that can release hydrogen sulfide when disturbed. A reading made while the hatch is closed describes that sampling condition; it cannot predict the worker's exposure when liquid circulates or the hatch opens. H2S can accumulate in low or poorly ventilated locations, but wind, turbulence and the release point also matter. Do not use gas density as a substitute for a site assessment. Smell cannot establish safety: odor perception can fail, and exposure can incapacitate a worker before a useful warning is recognized.

At a fictional well pad, the truck driver has a personal monitor, while a technician has the authorization and equipment to sample process hazards. Those roles differ. The driver checks the assigned monitor's readiness and wears it with unobstructed sampling access; the technician selects sensors, locations and interpretation criteria. Neither a normal oxygen reading nor a zero H2S reading establishes the absence of every flammable or toxic substance. A powered display is not proof that a sensor responds to gas.

Worked decision: circulation begins after the pre-job survey. Pause the hatch-opening step and obtain a reassessment of release pathways, controls and monitoring. Record the changed process state alongside the old result. Do not erase the old result or treat its timestamp as authorization for different work. The assessment should connect the actual task, suitable detector, ventilation, exclusion area and escape arrangements before exposure begins.`,[
 ['Circulation starts after a closed-hatch test. What happens before opening?','Reassess the changed release conditions.','Reuse the old reading for the shift.','Disable the alarm to prevent nuisance trips.','Ask the driver to smell the hatch.','Disturbance can change gas release; the earlier measurement did not represent the new operation.'],
 ['A zero H2S reading establishes which fact?','No detected target gas within that measurement’s limits.','Oxygen is adequate.','No toxic chemical is present.','The tank cannot release gas.','A sensor result is bounded by its target, location, condition and capability.'],
 ['A monitor display works but its required response check fails. What next?','Remove it from reliance and obtain a verified suitable unit.','Use it with a buddy.','Move its alarm setting upward.','Use odor as the backup sensor.','Power does not demonstrate sensor response.']]],
 ['Escape planning at the actual work position',`Escape planning starts before the crew approaches the exposure area. Locate the alarms, wind indicators, routes, alternative muster points and accountability contact. Walk the safe access route during the briefing when conditions permit. A route drawn on a map may be blocked by a parked trailer, a temporary hose or a locked gate. Confirm the alternative from the actual work position rather than memorizing only the main entrance.

Consider a fictional pad with the normal muster east of the tank battery. A release occurs while the wind carries the plume toward that point. Workers follow the emergency plan's safe alternate route and muster arrangement; they do not march through the suspected release because the familiar sign is visible. Escape direction must account for the release, terrain and current wind. Where needed, move crosswind out of the plume and continue to the designated safe area according to the site plan. Never enter a suspected gas area to retrieve a windsock, phone or coworker.

An alarm is an action signal, not an invitation to diagnose the detector while remaining exposed. Leave promptly, warn others without entering the hazard, and report from safety. Report the location, alarm, people potentially affected and last known location of missing persons. An absent odor or a silenced alarm is not clearance to return. Re-entry belongs to the designated authority after technical and emergency checks. If a prescribed escape respirator is part of the plan, its actual donning and use must already have been taught and demonstrated within its approved limitations.`,[
 ['The normal muster lies in the suspected plume. What is appropriate?','Use the plan’s safe alternate route and accountability point.','Go to the usual sign regardless.','Wait beside the tank for a second alarm.','Drive into the plume to collect coworkers.','Muster must remain safely accessible under the actual release conditions.'],
 ['The alarm stops after evacuation. Who clears return?','The designated site authority after required verification.','The first worker who hears silence.','The driver with the highest quiz score.','Anyone who cannot smell gas.','Alarm cessation does not establish safe re-entry.'],
 ['What should a worker communicate from safety?','Observed alarm, location and missing-person information.','An invented concentration.','A promise the tank is safe.','Only the delivery schedule.','Responders need observed facts rather than guesses.']]],
 ['Respirator selection and the rescue boundary',`Respirators do different jobs. A particulate filtering facepiece does not supply breathable air and is not protection against an H2S emergency. An air-purifying cartridge relies on a suitable atmosphere, an approved application and the employer's selection and change schedule. It does not create oxygen. Unknown concentrations and immediately dangerous atmospheres require the respiratory program's appropriate supplied-air protection and rescue arrangements. An escape-only device is for leaving; it does not authorize entry or continued work.

Required respirator users need the employer's medical evaluation before required fit testing or use, appropriate fit testing for tight-fitting facepieces and instruction on the actual equipment. A user seal check is a check made when the facepiece is donned; it does not replace the fit test. Facial hair or other interference at the sealing surface defeats the intended seal. The supervisor receives the permissible medical clearance information, not a worker's private medical history in the course record.

Worked case: a contractor sees a coworker collapse near a sour-water pit. Wearing a monitor and having completed this course do not make the contractor a rescuer. The correct action is emergency activation from safety and prevention of further unprotected entry. Rescue personnel need the actual protective equipment, training, communication and backup required by their assignment. Holding one's breath, wetting a cloth or attaching an escape hood cannot replace that system. Tell dispatch about the suspected atmosphere and last known position so responders can prepare without manufacturing a gas reading.`,[
 ['An escape-only device permits which action?','Leaving within its approved limitations.','Entering for rescue.','Completing a short job.','Testing an unknown atmosphere.','Escape equipment does not authorize entry or work.'],
 ['What replaces a required tight-fitting respirator fit test?','None of these substitutes.','A user seal check.','A purchase receipt.','A successful online quiz.','The fit test and daily use checks serve different purposes.'],
 ['A coworker collapses in a suspected H2S area. What is the unassigned worker’s response?','Activate rescue from safety and prevent unprotected entry.','Enter while holding breath.','Enter with only a personal detector.','Send the strongest coworker.','An unprotected rescue attempt can create additional casualties.']]],
 ['Demonstrating H2S readiness without live exposure',`Use a controlled exercise with a training monitor, a site sketch and the assigned escape equipment. Never release H2S or create a hazardous atmosphere to test a learner. The evaluator first confirms which duties are being assessed: general exposure awareness, personal-monitor use, escape-device use, gas testing and rescue are different authorizations. A pass for one must not silently grant the others.

The learner identifies an unblocked primary and alternate route, demonstrates the manufacturer's readiness checks, positions the monitor correctly and responds to a simulated alarm. Introduce a blocked gate or a changed wind direction. The learner must adapt through the site plan, communicate from safety and account for a missing person without entering the hazard. If escape-device use is assigned, assess the actual model with a suitable training unit and the manufacturer's procedure; do not consume or damage emergency stock casually.

Use an observable rubric. Passing requires correct readiness verification, recognition of an unusable unit, prompt escape, safe communication and no unauthorized return. Entering the simulated exclusion area to rescue, relying on smell, ignoring the alarm or using escape equipment to resume work is a critical failure. Stop the exercise, explain the failed decision, provide practice and reassess with a different scenario. Record the original result and the later outcome separately.

Record evaluator identity, date, location, equipment model, assessed duties and restrictions. A course score belongs in the learning record. An employer's signed authorization belongs in the role record. Neither record should state that a Veriforce credential or specialist rescue qualification was awarded by this online program.`,[
 ['Which drill condition is appropriate?','A simulated alarm in a controlled safe area.','A small deliberate H2S release.','An unknown pit atmosphere.','A live rescue attempt without backup.','Skills can be assessed without creating toxic exposure.'],
 ['Which action is a critical failure?','Crossing the exclusion boundary to attempt unprotected rescue.','Reporting a missing person.','Choosing a safe alternate route.','Rejecting a failed detector.','Unprotected entry defeats the principal life-saving boundary.'],
 ['What must the practical record identify?','Actual model, duties, evaluator and observed outcome.','Only the online score.','An automatic H2S Clear award.','A universal rescue qualification.','Practical evidence must be specific to the equipment and role actually assessed.']]]
]);

program('OG07',[osha('1910.146'),'https://www.osha.gov/confined-spaces/construction'],
 ['Classification and alternate procedures','Entry-team coordination','Emergency and termination cases','Role-specific practical rubric'],
 ['Employer space inventory, actual permits, rescue-service evaluation and observed role proficiency remain separate. Construction Subpart AA applicability must be determined.'],[
 ['Classify the space before selecting the entry route',`A confined space is large enough for bodily entry, has restricted entry or exit and is not designed for continuous occupancy. A permit space adds the relevant serious hazards, such as a hazardous atmosphere, engulfment, trapping configuration or another recognized serious safety or health hazard. A large open top does not by itself make a tank safe. Entry can begin when any part of the body breaks the opening plane; leaning through the hatch is not a harmless preliminary inspection.

Worked case: a mixing vessel has a low oxygen alarm history and an internal agitator. A supervisor proposes using ventilation alone because yesterday's readings were acceptable. Ventilation does not eliminate the agitator hazard. Determine the applicable program, isolate hazardous energy, assess engulfment and connected piping, and establish entry conditions. The special general-industry alternate procedure is not a blanket waiver for every ventilated space. Its eligibility and supporting evidence must be established under the standard. Similarly, reclassification requires the relevant hazards to be eliminated, not merely controlled by relying on a respirator or watchful attendant.

The host and contractor exchange known hazards, precautions and relevant experience. Identify who coordinates simultaneous activities: cleaning one vessel can affect another through connected piping. In construction, apply the construction confined-space framework rather than automatically copying the general-industry permit. The worker should be able to describe why the chosen entry route applies and identify who must resolve an uncertain classification before anyone crosses the opening.`,[
 ['A worker leans through a permit-space hatch. Is entry avoided?','No; crossing the opening plane can constitute entry.','Yes, if feet remain outside.','Yes, if no tools are used.','Yes, for under one minute.','Entry is not limited to placing the whole body inside.'],
 ['Ventilation controls atmosphere but an agitator remains energized. What follows?','The entry has unresolved mechanical hazards.','Ventilation authorizes entry.','The attendant can substitute for isolation.','An oxygen reading eliminates movement risk.','Atmospheric controls do not eliminate mechanical energy.'],
 ['Which fact is necessary before using an alternate entry procedure?','Evidence that its eligibility conditions are actually met.','A short task duration.','A supervisor’s confidence.','A signed attendance sheet.','Alternate procedures have specific conditions, not discretionary shortcuts.']]],
 ['Make entrant, attendant and supervisor actions fit together',`The entrant knows the hazards, uses assigned controls, maintains communication and exits when the conditions or instructions require. The attendant remains outside, tracks authorized entrants, recognizes conditions requiring evacuation and summons the designated rescue service. The entry supervisor verifies prerequisites, authorizes entry and terminates it when required. A person may hold more than one role only when trained and able to perform every assigned duty without conflict.

Before entry, walk through a communication failure. What does a missed response mean, how does the attendant order evacuation, and how is rescue summoned from this location? A radio that works at the gate may fail behind a steel vessel. Establish the actual communication method and backup. The attendant must not take a delivery call that diverts attention from entrants. Monitoring multiple spaces is acceptable only when the duties remain effectively performable under the employer's procedure; proximity alone is not proof.

Worked case: the attendant is asked to move a truck while two workers remain inside. The correct action is a controlled transfer to a trained, briefed replacement or withdrawal of the entrants before leaving the post. A clipboard on a chair does not transfer responsibility. The replacement needs entrant count, current conditions, alarms, communication arrangements and rescue contacts. On a change in acceptable conditions, the entrant exits and the supervisor addresses the cause; the team does not renegotiate alarm criteria while workers remain exposed.`,[
 ['The attendant must leave to move a truck. What is acceptable?','A proper trained handover or entrant withdrawal first.','Leave the radio on a chair.','Ask an entrant to self-monitor.','Leave for a few minutes without notice.','Attendant duties must remain continuously effective.'],
 ['Where should communication be demonstrated?','At the actual entry interface and work conditions.','Only at the office.','Only in a classroom.','Only with the radio charging.','Steel structures and distance can make an otherwise functional radio unsuitable.'],
 ['An entrant loses required communication. What should the plan provide?','A defined withdrawal and emergency response.','Automatic permission to finish.','A higher exposure limit.','Permission to disable alarms.','Loss of a required safeguard must have a preplanned response.']]],
 ['Rescue readiness and permit termination',`A telephone number is not a rescue plan. Evaluate whether the designated service can respond in time for the identified hazards, reach the entry point, access the space and perform the needed rescue. An emergency service may be capable generally but unavailable or unequipped for a particular vessel. Share relevant hazards and permit access so the service can plan and practice. Where employee rescue duties are assigned, the required training, equipment, practice and first-aid provisions must be met.

Non-entry retrieval is planned where required and suitable; it is not simply attaching a line without considering entanglement, internal obstacles or the person's path through the opening. The team must know when retrieval is appropriate and who operates it. An attendant cannot abandon their protective duties and enter impulsively. Rescue entry requires its own trained personnel and safeguards.

Worked case: during cleaning, the atmosphere alarm activates and an entrant reports dizziness. Order immediate exit, summon the designated rescue service and provide location and hazard information. Perform only the trained actions that are safe from outside. If the team exits, do not restart just because ventilation lowers the reading. Cancel or suspend the permit as required by the applicable program, investigate the change and reestablish conditions through proper authorization.

Preserve the permit and abnormal conditions for program review. In the general-industry permit program, canceled permits are retained for at least one year to support review. A clean replacement permit must not conceal the earlier alarm or evacuation.`,[
 ['What makes rescue arrangements credible?','Verified capability, access and timely availability for this space.','A generic phone number alone.','An untrained standby coworker.','A promise to improvise.','Rescue suitability is specific to the space and hazards.'],
 ['After evacuation, ventilation restores a reading. What is still needed?','Investigation and proper reauthorization under the entry program.','Immediate unsupervised return.','Deletion of the alarm.','Only a new quiz score.','The cause and all entry conditions must be addressed.'],
 ['Why preserve canceled general-industry permits?','They support the required program review and hazard learning.','They replace rescue practice.','They authorize next year’s entry.','They eliminate isolation checks.','Retained permits document what actually occurred.']]],
 ['A role-based entry-team evaluation',`Set up a nonhazardous mock entry with a vessel drawing, an energy-isolation record, a sample permit and simulated readings. Each participant must demonstrate the assigned role. An entrant should identify the limits, inspect assigned equipment, communicate and withdraw on command. An attendant should keep an accurate count, recognize a prohibited condition, order evacuation and contact rescue. An entry supervisor should find missing prerequisites and refuse an incomplete permit.

Introduce two changes separately: an unexplained reading and a contractor opening a connected line. Ask the learner to explain how each change could invalidate the established conditions. Passing requires the correct stop, communication and authorization sequence, not merely naming a hazard. Do not use a real oxygen-deficient space as a testing venue. A mock retrieval may use a training dummy and suitable equipment under competent supervision; it must not expose a learner to a fall, suspended-load or entanglement hazard.

The rubric records classification reasoning, isolation interface, test interpretation, role discipline, communication and emergency action. Unsafe entry, abandoning the attendant post, inventing a gas result or treating a rescue phone number as proven capability is a critical failure. The evaluator stops the exercise, teaches the missed step and conducts another scenario. Preserve the initial failed result.

The employer retains the employee name, trainer identification and training date and makes the certification available as required. Record separately any rescue practice and site authorization. This online course can establish knowledge completion; it cannot assert that the learner performed these physical tasks or that an external rescue service is available.`,[
 ['Which supervisor behavior demonstrates proficiency?','Rejecting a permit with unresolved connected-line hazards.','Signing every completed form.','Treating readings as isolation proof.','Delegating all decisions to entrants.','Supervision includes determining that prerequisites are truly satisfied.'],
 ['Which practical result must remain visible after remediation?','The original failed performance and the reassessment.','Only the latest passing result.','An invented first-time pass.','No evaluator observations.','Preserving both outcomes prevents remediation from falsifying history.'],
 ['An online score alone proves what?','Assessed knowledge, not observed entry or rescue performance.','Rescue-service capability.','Space-specific entry authorization.','Practical proficiency for every role.','Physical performance and employer authorization require separate evidence.']]]
]);
